WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.290
- Hi, everyone thank you for joining.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:11.620
And we're very happy to have
Dr. Catherine Briddick from

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.460
the Refugee Study Center,

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:14.930
the Department of International
Development at the

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:17.330
University of Oxford in the UK joining us.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:21.890
Thank you for joining us
virtually Dr. Briddick and we'll,

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:24.430
we've had more than a year long

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:26.700
seminar series,

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.370
and today we'll explore a
new aspect of this, which is

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:33.950
the intersection of gender and migration.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.590
So we're very excited to hear you speak.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.283
So not only will we get this in,

00:00:43.678 --> 00:00:46.860
in live today, we're
also recording the event.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.570
We'll put it up on our webpage, so

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:51.013
others will have access
to it offline as well.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:53.253
So, okay.

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:54.200
I'll turn it over to you.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.220
- Thank you very much for
the invitation to speak today

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:01.860
after what has been,
I think for many of us

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.450
kind of demanding time,

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.890
I appreciate there are
many calls on your time.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.770
So thank you very much for
listening to me talk today

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:11.430
about CEDAW and discrimination experienced

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.523
in the context of migration control.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.970
So women are disadvantaged
by migration law

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:24.260
in a number of different ways.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:27.180
Disadvantage can be the
product of rules or practices,

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:30.860
which explicitly treat women
less favorably than men.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:34.220
So for example, rules that
prevent women's emigration.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.510
Usually however, gender
disadvantage flows from rules,

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:41.310
which appear on their face
to be gender or sex neutral,

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:42.890
but which actually disadvantage

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:45.500
women or particular groups of women.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.660
The disadvantage can be
expressive or material,

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.460
it can entail stereotyping, the
devaluation of women's work,

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.850
loss of employment or other opportunities

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:56.080
and the confinement of women

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.540
to certain roles in a relationship.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:01.250
It can increase women's risk
of experiencing violence,

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.120
and it can simply be violent.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:05.390
Now, such rules and practices

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:08.380
can be characterized as being
unlawfully discriminatory

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:10.840
by reference to national
equality legislation

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.930
or international law.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.370
Over the next 45 minutes,

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:15.940
I'm going to be presenting research,

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.990
which examines the application
of the UN Convention

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:21.590
on the Elimination of all
Forms of Discrimination

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:23.147
Against Women, CEDAW.

00:02:24.091 --> 00:02:27.000
And I'm going to be asking
two broad questions.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.570
Firstly, what does CEDAW's

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:30.980
general prohibition of
discrimination entail

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.970
in the context of migration control?

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:38.160
Put another way, what detailed
obligations do states assume

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.940
under CEDAW in relation to then
migration control functions?

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.070
And second, when rules and
practices are challenged

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:47.110
on the grounds of discrimination,

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:48.640
what outcome do they have

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.490
for the women who bring those challenges?

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:52.880
In the second part of the research,

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.430
then I'm moving to look
at the implementation

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:58.540
of the standards that the
CEDAW committee has set

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:01.260
and I that do by reviewing
individual complaints

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.940
that are brought under
CEDAW's optional protocol

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.813
over a nine year period.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.450
As will hopefully be apparent,

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:10.670
the seminar therefore is rooted

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:12.600
in a legal disciplinary approach

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.430
to both discrimination and migration law.

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:18.240
And I appreciate that many of
the people joining me today

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.590
are from a multidisciplinary program.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:24.300
So if you have any questions
or comments as we go,

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.540
please do ask them in
the chat and stop me,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.570
it's much better to
clarify a term or a concept

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:34.120
as I unpack, hopefully what
will be my central argument.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.563
And it should enable us to have hopefully

00:03:35.563 --> 00:03:37.790
a more fruitful exchange of ideas

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:39.403
at the end of the presentation.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.643
So I'm going to be focusing,
as I said on CEDAW,

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:48.840
and I'm going to be examining
the work of the treaty body,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.690
the human rights treaty body that oversees

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:55.283
the conventions implementation,
the CEDAW committee.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:59.470
So I'm going to do three things.

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:00.760
Firstly, I'm going to explain what

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:04.090
discrimination against women
is in international law,

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:06.490
secondly, I'm going to turn to

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:09.030
discuss what the convention requires

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.470
in the migration control context

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:13.190
and third I'm going to examine

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:16.220
the committees applications
of the obligations

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:19.780
it has delineated to states.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:22.100
There are two parts of my argument.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:25.180
The first is that I'm
going to identify doctrinal

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:27.930
and empirical shortcomings
in the committee's reasoning

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:31.220
on when and why certain widespread

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:35.203
migration control practices
are unlawfully discriminatory.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.470
And second, when I turn
to looking at individual

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.960
communications brought
under the conventions

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.250
optional protocol, I'm
going to reveal a particular

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:47.320
kind of responsibility deficit.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:50.140
A deficit between
determination and application,

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:52.070
between how the committee approaches

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:54.460
migrant women's rights in the abstract

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.890
when discharging its
general interpretive role

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:58.600
and when it behaves

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.410
and when confronted with a
particular rule or practice

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.963
that an individual
alleges is discriminatory.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:09.050
So turning to the first of these.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:10.540
As maybe apparent from the slide,

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:12.810
I have primary school aged children,

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:15.950
which means I do a lot of coloring in.

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:20.740
So in front of you, you
have the article one

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:24.390
definition of discrimination against women

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:28.220
that's found right at the
outset of the CEDAW convention.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.480
And as you can see this is an asymmetrical

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.263
protection from discrimination.

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:36.840
So unlike CEDAW, sorry,
unlike CERD, for example,

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.990
which prohibits, which
defines and then prohibits

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:41.550
any forms of discrimination
on the grounds of race,

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.933
CEDAW specifically protects
women from discrimination.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:48.560
And at first glance, if we
look at this definition,

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:50.460
CEDAW seems ideally placed

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:52.270
to respond to the discrimination

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:55.640
that migrant women may be subject to.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:58.360
Article one encompasses both direct

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.700
and indirect discrimination.

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:02.900
It may therefore be relied
on to challenge rules,

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:06.340
which are explicitly
disadvantageous to women

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:08.020
and those which negatively affect women

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:09.300
or particular groups of women,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.603
even though they appear
to be gender neutral.

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:17.620
The wording that I have picked
out in, I think it is yellow.

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:23.100
Any distinction,
exclusion, or restriction,

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:26.230
which has the effect or
purpose of moving into green,

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:28.830
establishes adverse effect,

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:31.080
whether or not that is intentional

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:34.920
as being the definitions,
crucial criteria.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:38.080
The breadth of the
definition and in particular,

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:43.080
it's reference to language
that refers to public life.

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:46.050
So the prohibition of
discrimination that occurs

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:48.310
and economic, social, cultural, civil,

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:52.020
or any other field enables the convention

00:06:54.285 --> 00:06:56.260
to non-refoulement essentially

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:59.010
to provide a role in the
convention that includes

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:00.470
gender based violence.

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:02.380
That is violence that is directed against

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:03.870
a woman because she is a woman

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:06.990
or which affects women disproportionately.

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:09.420
Now states migration control practices

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:10.700
such as pushbacks, for example,

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:13.160
may involve violence or be violence,

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:16.360
but there are other practices
that states engage in

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:18.950
such as making people
destitute for example,

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:21.130
or prohibited women from having recourse

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:24.180
to certain state services,
such as healthcare.

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:26.560
These services may increase
women's vulnerability

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:30.470
to the violence that's
perpetrated by non-state actors.

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:32.530
Now the CEDAW committees characterization

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:35.210
as gender-based violence as
a form of discrimination,

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:37.970
enables such rules and
practices to be challenged,

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:40.563
to be brought within the
purview of the convention.

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:44.330
Finally, article one can be relied on

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:46.550
to reinforce and compliment

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:48.970
two legal regimes, which are crucial

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:50.400
to international migration,

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:53.290
but whose key instruments fail to define

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:56.140
or prohibit sex or gender discrimination,

00:07:56.140 --> 00:08:00.120
the trafficking and
international protection regimes.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:02.120
The definition also requires states

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:05.030
to provide gender
sensitive interpretations

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:07.093
of those instruments.

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:11.760
Now, while the International Covenant

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.750
on Civil and Political Rights, the ICCPR

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:16.230
and the Convention on the Elimination

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:18.800
of all forms of Racial
Discrimination, CERD

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:22.350
differentiate between
citizens and non-citizens,

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:25.395
the CEDAW committee is
clear that the obligations

00:08:25.395 --> 00:08:29.140
of state parties apply
without discrimination

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:32.610
to both citizens and
non-citizens including refugees,

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:35.070
asylum seekers, migrant workers,

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:37.130
and stateless persons
within their territory

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:38.720
or effective control

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:41.750
even if not situated within the territory.

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:44.930
States parties are responsible
for all their actions,

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:46.370
the committee appoints,

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:49.853
whether or not the individual
consent is in their territory.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:52.250
Now the CEDAW committee,

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:54.700
yes.
- Catherine, sorry a question.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:57.380
So who is in charge of enforcing

00:09:00.961 --> 00:09:02.073
these articles?

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:05.163
- So the enforcement-

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:06.863
Yes.

00:09:06.863 --> 00:09:09.350
So the CEDAW committee is the treaty body

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:10.630
responsible for monitoring

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:13.220
the application of the convention.

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:16.060
Now the CEDAW committee has two roles.

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:17.750
So one is the world I mentioned earlier,

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:19.990
it hears individual complaints.

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:22.930
So that's where individuals
can allege that for example,

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:24.610
they have been discriminated against

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:27.300
contrary to article one of the convention

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:29.780
and the CEDAW committee
will adjudicate on that

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:31.780
much like an international
court or tribunal,

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:33.267
although it's not that.

00:09:33.267 --> 00:09:38.267
But the CEDAW committee will
also review reports of states.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:41.300
So states party to CEDAW
have to produce information

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:43.210
to the committee every four or five years

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:45.730
to talk about how they've
implemented the convention

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:47.220
in their domestic law.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:49.450
And there's also a role
for civil society there

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:52.910
and pointing out or highlighting gaps in

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:55.940
state's implementation of the convention.

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:57.440
And we might want to think about

00:09:57.440 --> 00:09:59.760
the CEDAW committee as being a soft court.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:02.000
So it's not an international
court or tribunal

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:03.977
like the Inter-American court, for example

00:10:03.977 --> 00:10:07.063
but it has this monitoring
and adjudicative role.

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:12.960
- And how many states have signing into,

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:17.110
I mean, this, I mean,
complying of this convention?

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:20.840
- So I think there are over

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:23.490
a hundred states parties to CEDAW.

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:24.960
It's one of the most

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:30.950
widely ratified of the conventions.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:33.233
So

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:36.310
it's part of the international
bill of rights, essentially.

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:39.550
Is part of the kind of core framework

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:42.220
for dealing with
international human rights law

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:43.860
and international rights abuses.

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:45.980
The reason why I'm sounding hesitant

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:48.240
is because while CEDAW has
a really important role

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:49.330
in international human rights,

00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:52.140
so it's also the most reserved treaty.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:54.260
So what that means is that states sign

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:55.390
and ratify the convention,

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:58.810
but they also enter reservations
to particular parts of it,

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:01.310
which means that they try and argue

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:02.700
that either they shouldn't be bound

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:06.360
by a particular provision,
such as one consent

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:08.439
with the quality of marriage, for example.

00:11:08.439 --> 00:11:09.930
Or that they have a particularly

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:12.860
or slightly different
interpretation of it.

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:15.420
So it's a convention
that's ratified by states

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:20.420
across the world, but it's also
one of the conventions that

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:23.680
states try and take almost like
a pick and mix approach to,

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:24.730
they are highly selective

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:26.580
in the legal obligations they assume.

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:33.810
Now, the reason why I've got this quote

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:35.740
on the slide in front of you is because

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:38.960
a number of human rights
instruments in international law

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:42.020
differentiate between the
rights that accrue to citizens

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:44.090
and the rights that accrue to migrants

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:46.530
and CEDAW takes a different approach.

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:48.510
It takes a broad approach to both

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:51.570
its beneficiary group and jurisdiction.

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:54.650
So the rights that it provides
should apply to women,

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:56.720
whether they have a
secure migration stages

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:00.001
in the country that they are
in, or whether they don't,

00:12:00.001 --> 00:12:01.660
and that's quite different

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:04.800
from other international instruments.

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:07.360
CEDAW's also establishing here in effects

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:09.290
based approach to jurisdiction.

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:11.600
So it can protect or offers protection

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:15.203
to individuals from harm
that crosses state borders.

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:21.410
So the provisional title of
my talk today was when does

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:24.563
migration law discriminate against women.

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:27.570
And I've mentioned that the
first part of the research

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:32.370
was focusing on working out
what state's obligations were

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:34.120
under international human rights law

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:37.710
in relation to their
migration control functions.

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:41.790
So article one of CEDAW defines
discrimination against women

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:45.730
and article two of CEDAW prohibits it.

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:47.530
And the committee that monitors it,

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:49.820
the treaty body that I must mentioned

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:51.610
draws on article one and two

00:12:51.610 --> 00:12:54.070
to impugn as unlawfully discriminatory

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:57.273
a number of states
migration control practices.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:02.260
In a moment I'm going to talk
to you about non-refoulement.

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:04.120
The protection that's
offered to individuals

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:06.970
who can't be returned to
their country of origin,

00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:09.320
because for example, they face

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:11.840
serious forms of discrimination or harm,

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:13.900
including gender based violence.

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:15.849
But in an article for the American

00:13:15.849 --> 00:13:17.640
Journal of International law,

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:21.579
I go into a lot more detail
about the way that, for example,

00:13:21.579 --> 00:13:24.440
certain migration control practices,

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:27.530
which are widely used by
states, for example, detention

00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:32.040
or pushback are impugned by
the committee as engaging it's

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:34.560
prohibition on discrimination.

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:37.360
A number of rules, which
are very commonly found in,

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:39.970
for example, British
and US immigration law

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:42.330
are also highlighted by the committee

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:45.580
as engaging in indirect discrimination.

00:13:45.580 --> 00:13:49.440
And these are rules that for
example, mega migrants spouse

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:53.550
dependent on their partner or rules,

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:55.440
which mean that if someone wants to apply

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:56.770
for refugee family reunion,

00:13:56.770 --> 00:14:00.230
they have to meet a certain
minimum income requirement.

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:01.710
Now, these rules on their face

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:03.230
may appear to be gender neutral,

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:05.860
but they have particular
gendered consequences.

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:08.210
So women might find it more
difficult than men, for example,

00:14:08.210 --> 00:14:10.150
to meet minimum income requirements

00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:13.240
because of gender disadvantage
in the labor market.

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:15.850
And similarly, women who are placed

00:14:15.850 --> 00:14:17.410
or who are forced into relationships

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:20.000
of dependency with an employer or a spouse

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:23.560
may face an increased risk
of being subject to violence.

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:26.170
So the broader research
project that I'm involved in

00:14:26.170 --> 00:14:28.410
go through states migration laws

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:30.840
in a much more fine-grained way

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:32.520
and identifies ways in which those

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:34.320
rules are unlawfully discriminatory.

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:37.150
We might though today be more interested

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:39.510
in how the CEDAW committee
offers protections

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:42.690
to refugees, and those who are in need

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:44.223
of international protection.

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:48.010
Now, the CEDAW committee has produced

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:49.870
a general recommendation.

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:52.840
On the gender-related
dimension of refugee status,

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:56.670
asylum nationality, and
the statelessness of women.

00:14:56.670 --> 00:15:00.790
Now general recommendations
are an interpretive tool,

00:15:00.790 --> 00:15:03.330
they're produced by the CEDAW committee

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:06.780
and they aim to unpack the core

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:09.940
rights that are protected
by the convention.

00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:12.570
They tell states essentially
how they need to meet their

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:16.830
legal obligations and they can
be relied on by individuals

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:20.340
to understand when and their
rights have been violated

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:22.543
and what the appropriate remedies may be.

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:26.030
So I'm going to talk
about the way that the

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:28.470
CEDAW committee has done
some non-refoulement.

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:29.537
Now, I've already mentioned that

00:15:29.537 --> 00:15:31.750
the CEDAW committee's definition

00:15:31.750 --> 00:15:34.480
of discrimination against women

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:37.417
includes gender based violence.

00:15:37.417 --> 00:15:38.976
And I've said, had this non-refoulement

00:15:38.976 --> 00:15:40.200
where it was particularly important

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:42.830
because key instruments, in for example,

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:46.680
the international protection
regime don't prohibit

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:48.740
discrimination against women.

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:50.740
But there are other gaps or absences

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:53.360
in the international protection regime.

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:57.670
So you might be very well
familiar with article 1.A.(2)

00:15:57.670 --> 00:16:00.040
definition of a refugee that can be found

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:03.650
in the convention relating
to the status of refugees.

00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:06.000
And it states that
individuals are refugees

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:08.380
if they have a well-founded
fear of being persecuted,

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:10.930
because for example of their nationality

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:12.970
or their political opinion.

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:15.520
Now, whilst nationality
political opinion and race

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:18.400
are all protected grounds
in the refugee convention,

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:21.710
there isn't a ground that
relates to sex, gender,

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:25.060
sexual orientation, or gender identity.

00:16:25.060 --> 00:16:27.360
And instead, individuals who seek to rely

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:28.980
on the refugee convention

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:31.620
have to bring their claim
within the final ground

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:34.013
that set out in the
article 1.A.(2) definition

00:16:34.013 --> 00:16:36.640
that of particular social group.

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:38.920
Now I can talk for hours and
I'm not going to do so now

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:42.890
about the problems with the
jurisprudence on the PSG ground.

00:16:42.890 --> 00:16:45.200
And in the Q&amp;A we might
want to think about

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:47.340
particular problems in the US context

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:49.420
with the very restrictive interpretation

00:16:49.420 --> 00:16:51.460
that's used by US courts and tribunals,

00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:55.450
one, which is very much out of
step with international law.

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:58.200
What's really important
for us today though,

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:01.080
is the role that the
CEDAW committee has taken

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:03.000
into arguing that states need to take

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:06.440
a gender sensitive approach
to all of the convention

00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:09.060
grounds, not just the PSG ground,

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:12.350
but for example, recognizing
women's political activism

00:17:12.350 --> 00:17:15.890
and women's political agency
and protecting, for example,

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:18.260
feminist resistance to domestic violence

00:17:18.260 --> 00:17:20.260
or feminist resistance to trafficking

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:22.860
as political opinions and not just

00:17:22.860 --> 00:17:26.110
dealing with those cases
under the PSG ground.

00:17:26.110 --> 00:17:28.040
And it's not just a
gender sensitive approach

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:31.550
to credibility that states
are required to take

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:34.050
it's about internal relocation

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:36.470
and all aspects of the refugee convention

00:17:36.470 --> 00:17:37.513
definition as well.

00:17:40.090 --> 00:17:43.140
In addition to putting forward
a gender sensitive approach

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:46.850
to the refugee convention,

00:17:46.850 --> 00:17:48.860
the CEDAW committee goes
into a lot of detail

00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:51.390
as to what refugee status determination

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:53.410
should look like for women.

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:56.190
And this is important
because this is another gap

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:58.560
in the article in that CSR.

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:00.500
So the refugee convention tells us

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:03.665
in article 1.A.(2) who a refugee is,

00:18:03.665 --> 00:18:04.930
but the refugee convention doesn't tell us

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:07.250
how we should recognize refugees.

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:09.670
It doesn't tell us what
rights refugees have

00:18:09.670 --> 00:18:11.380
when they're going through
status determination.

00:18:11.380 --> 00:18:13.958
Do they have a right to
a lawyer for example.

00:18:13.958 --> 00:18:16.180
And what particular rights
do vulnerable groups

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:18.473
going through this process require.

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:21.570
Now this general
recommendation makes it clear

00:18:21.570 --> 00:18:22.880
that protection seeking women

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:25.110
should have their claims
determined by a system,

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:27.380
which is informed in every respect,

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:30.710
by a thorough understanding
of discrimination

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:33.440
and persecution on the
grounds of sex and gender

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:35.950
and the impacts of that treatment.

00:18:35.950 --> 00:18:38.290
And specific examples of this gender

00:18:38.290 --> 00:18:40.540
sensitive approach would be, for example,

00:18:40.540 --> 00:18:44.040
requiring a woman to be
interviewed by a caseworker

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:47.710
who is also a woman and having
a female interpreter present.

00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:50.410
It includes things like
providing childcare

00:18:50.410 --> 00:18:53.180
and ensuring that victims of
torture and sexual violence

00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:54.750
are referred to and receive

00:18:54.750 --> 00:18:57.870
appropriate psychosocial support.

00:18:57.870 --> 00:19:00.850
So these protections are
really important to ensure

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:03.610
that the gender sensitive
interpretation of the convention

00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:07.073
is actually available to women
who go through RSD processes.

00:19:08.150 --> 00:19:11.900
The core protection though,
general recommendation 32,

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:14.790
what this particular interpretation offers

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:18.520
is that women can use
CEDAW and rely on CEDAW

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:20.700
to argue that they should not be returned

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:23.140
to situations where
they face, for example,

00:19:23.140 --> 00:19:25.230
discrimination on the grounds of sex

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:28.320
or gender based violence
and CEDAW's protection

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:30.290
from refoulement from this return

00:19:30.290 --> 00:19:31.710
is broader than the protection

00:19:31.710 --> 00:19:34.410
that applies to refugees
under the refugee convention.

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:40.470
So CEDAW's expansive
definition of discrimination

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:42.170
can be used to identify

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:43.437
unlawful discrimination against them

00:19:43.437 --> 00:19:45.350
and at every stage in their journey

00:19:45.350 --> 00:19:46.420
from countries of origin,

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:49.170
to countries of destination and beyond.

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:50.910
And CEDAW committee recommendations,

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:52.860
like the one that I've
just talked you through

00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:55.710
when applied indivisibly establishes a set

00:19:55.710 --> 00:19:58.250
of mostly reinforcing obligations,

00:19:58.250 --> 00:20:00.170
which requires states to remedy

00:20:00.170 --> 00:20:02.143
discriminatory treatment against women.

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:05.870
There are however, a number of
failures and a number of gaps

00:20:05.870 --> 00:20:08.870
and emissions in the committees reasoning.

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:11.340
So when it comes to indirect
discrimination, for example,

00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:14.820
the committee doesn't tell
women how they can evidence

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:15.960
differential treatment,

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:18.380
do they have to do so on the
basis of statistical evidence

00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:20.180
or not, for example.

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:22.330
And crucially, the CEDAW committee

00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:24.770
is silent on questions of justification.

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:26.960
And this is really important
from a discrimination

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:31.300
law perspective because
states often assert

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:34.020
particular rights as flowing
from their sovereignty

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:36.650
in the cases of migration control.

00:20:36.650 --> 00:20:39.500
They will have kind of
very broad statements that

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:41.150
the progressives of migration control

00:20:41.150 --> 00:20:44.430
requires things like detention,

00:20:44.430 --> 00:20:48.430
deportation, pushbacks, or
variety of other practices.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:49.740
Now, if these practices

00:20:49.740 --> 00:20:51.870
have disproportionate impacts on women,

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:53.360
the legal question that we have to ask

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:56.370
is whether these disproportional
impacts can be justified

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:58.300
and we need the committee
to be really clear

00:20:58.300 --> 00:21:00.630
on the circumstances in which states

00:21:00.630 --> 00:21:03.110
are entitled to pursue those measures

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:06.180
and the circumstances where adverse effect

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:10.070
to use the language of US
discrimination law is not

00:21:10.070 --> 00:21:12.210
justifiable treatment
and therefore the state

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:14.730
should change its policies.

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:16.340
And I think if you're
interested in kind of

00:21:16.340 --> 00:21:18.670
following this line of argument further,

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:21.570
the CEDAW committee has
produced a general comment

00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:25.840
specifically on trafficking
and global migration control.

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:29.800
And this has a number of
really significant gaps in it

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:32.630
and essentially is reads
more like a policy statement

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:34.910
than a legal tool that
individuals could use

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:37.300
if they have been trafficked, for example,

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:39.360
to argue for their rights against states

00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:42.630
or for those involved in
civil society organizations,

00:21:42.630 --> 00:21:44.980
to understand what, for example,

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:48.110
a process that's able to
identify victims of trafficking

00:21:48.110 --> 00:21:49.023
should look like.

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:54.600
I'm now going to move
to the kind of second

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:55.940
parts of my research

00:21:55.940 --> 00:21:58.970
and discuss CEDAW committee jurisprudence.

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:02.760
So this is about cases that
are brought to the committee.

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:04.970
And cases can be brought by individuals

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:06.020
who are in countries,

00:22:06.020 --> 00:22:09.183
which have signed up to
CEDAW's optional protocol.

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:11.410
And for this research,

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:15.700
I reviewed nine years
worth of communications

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:18.330
and I identified 34 communications

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:21.670
as concerning states
migration control practices.

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:23.520
And the majority of these communications

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:25.520
were found to be inadmissible.

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:29.700
And I'll explain why some
of that was in a moment.

00:22:29.700 --> 00:22:33.290
There were only three cases
where a breach was found.

00:22:33.290 --> 00:22:35.900
Now, most of these cases
came from just one country

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:39.070
in Europe, Denmark and more
authors of communications

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:40.290
were represented by one man

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:44.107
then came from the remaining
mostly European countries.

00:22:44.107 --> 00:22:46.700
All of these cases or the
majority of these cases

00:22:46.700 --> 00:22:48.540
concerned non-refoulement.

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:50.310
So the protection of women who argued

00:22:50.310 --> 00:22:51.880
that they should not be returned

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:54.970
to countries where they
faced discrimination,

00:22:54.970 --> 00:22:58.470
persecution, and gender-based violence.

00:22:58.470 --> 00:23:02.940
Now these findings are significant
for a number of reasons.

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:04.770
The first I'm going to highlight

00:23:04.770 --> 00:23:06.840
is the fact that they do not represent

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:09.580
the breadth of the
committee's standard setting

00:23:09.580 --> 00:23:11.860
in relation to migration control.

00:23:11.860 --> 00:23:14.680
So both committees work
on direct discrimination

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:16.410
and indirect discrimination,

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:19.490
its protection of migrant
workers, for example,

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:21.950
and its protections for
people who are experiencing

00:23:21.950 --> 00:23:23.730
or at risk of gender based violence,

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:27.300
much of that is just absent
from these communications.

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:30.250
And instead, what we get,
if we look at CEDAW's work

00:23:30.250 --> 00:23:32.340
on migration control is a data set,

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:34.100
which is geographically skewed

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:36.240
to particular European countries,

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:38.833
and which is substantively distorted.

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:44.150
But there are two significant deficiencies

00:23:44.150 --> 00:23:46.520
in the way that the
committee has approached

00:23:46.520 --> 00:23:49.213
issues around migration control.

00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:52.460
And the first is around

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:57.450
its assessment of a
communications admissibility

00:23:57.450 --> 00:23:59.580
and the difference between admissibility

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:01.203
and merits determination.

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:04.200
And the second is that the committee adopt

00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:07.130
such a deferential standard of review,

00:24:07.130 --> 00:24:09.610
in some of the cases it's
called upon to decide

00:24:09.610 --> 00:24:12.760
that effectively fails
to apply the obligations

00:24:12.760 --> 00:24:14.313
it has delineated.

00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:17.760
So when you bring a case

00:24:17.760 --> 00:24:19.640
before an international court or tribunal,

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:20.780
you have to show that you

00:24:20.780 --> 00:24:24.290
meet certain admissibility requirements.

00:24:24.290 --> 00:24:27.230
You have to show that
you have, for example,

00:24:27.230 --> 00:24:29.280
exhausted domestic remedies.

00:24:29.280 --> 00:24:31.200
And that the thing that
you're complaining about

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:34.453
falls within the scope of
the relevant convention.

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:38.490
So when courts or human
rights treaty bodies,

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:43.310
like the CEDAW committee reviews
individual communications,

00:24:43.310 --> 00:24:44.500
the assessment it goes through

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:46.580
is often a two-stage assessment.

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:48.470
First, they have to ask themselves

00:24:48.470 --> 00:24:52.170
if a complaint is
admissible and then second

00:24:52.170 --> 00:24:54.150
they move to considering its merits.

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:57.950
Has there been a violation
of the relevant right

00:24:57.950 --> 00:25:00.343
that's protected or rights
that it's protected.

00:25:01.210 --> 00:25:04.500
Now in the first set of
cases that I've reviewed,

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:09.150
the committee makes these
points and decides these points,

00:25:09.150 --> 00:25:11.380
sequentially and appropriately.

00:25:11.380 --> 00:25:14.460
In the second set of cases
concerning non-refoulement,

00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:17.890
the committee collapses these
two questions into each other.

00:25:17.890 --> 00:25:19.923
And what that does is
it leaves individuals

00:25:19.923 --> 00:25:22.160
who are concerned about refoulement

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:25.530
at risk of return to
situations where they might be

00:25:25.530 --> 00:25:27.290
experiencing persecution

00:25:27.290 --> 00:25:29.513
or serious forms of gender-based violence.

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:32.320
The second problem the committee has

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:34.860
is around the standard of
review that it applies,

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:37.490
how it scrutinizes states reasoning

00:25:37.490 --> 00:25:40.310
in asylum determination processes.

00:25:40.310 --> 00:25:42.030
And my concern here is that the committee

00:25:42.030 --> 00:25:44.830
adopt such a deferential
standard of review,

00:25:44.830 --> 00:25:47.530
that it fails to protect

00:25:47.530 --> 00:25:50.653
individuals who seek to
rely on its obligations.

00:25:52.400 --> 00:25:56.700
So I'm going to talk about
these two deficiencies

00:25:56.700 --> 00:26:01.203
in the analysis by drawing
on for illustrative cases.

00:26:02.320 --> 00:26:05.940
So the first case that
I want to talk about

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:09.440
is RSAA versus Denmark.

00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:13.960
So RSAA was a Palestinian refugee.

00:26:13.960 --> 00:26:17.740
She was from Syria and she
obtained a Jordanian passport

00:26:17.740 --> 00:26:20.730
following her marriage to a Jordanian man.

00:26:20.730 --> 00:26:23.200
She had two children and she claimed

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:25.080
international protection in Denmark

00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:28.430
on the grounds that she had
experienced domestic violence,

00:26:28.430 --> 00:26:31.280
and that her children were
at risk of forced marriage.

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:33.810
Now, all of these things are protected

00:26:33.810 --> 00:26:37.403
or should be within the purview
of the CEDAW convention.

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:41.150
Her claim for asylum was however refused

00:26:41.150 --> 00:26:43.090
primarily on credibility grounds

00:26:43.090 --> 00:26:46.563
and her and her children's
deportation was arranged.

00:26:47.690 --> 00:26:49.460
So the CEDAW committee first looked

00:26:49.460 --> 00:26:52.630
at the admissibility of her complaint

00:26:52.630 --> 00:26:54.320
and under the CEDAW committees

00:26:54.320 --> 00:26:57.330
optional protocol, article 4(2)

00:26:57.330 --> 00:27:01.040
communication is admissible if it is

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:04.333
properly founded and
properly substantiated.

00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:08.690
Now the committee reviewed her case

00:27:08.690 --> 00:27:10.200
and found that her communication

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:12.810
was sufficiently substantiated.

00:27:12.810 --> 00:27:15.130
She'd been able to show
them on a prima facie basis

00:27:15.130 --> 00:27:19.363
that her CEDAW or protected
rights had been violated.

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:21.840
It then went on to consider

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:25.530
the substance of her
asylum claim, essentially

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:28.500
on a case by case basis
and applied the correct

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:29.900
merit standard of review,

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:32.320
which I've put on the
slide in front of you.

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:34.560
So the committee says that
it's for the authorities

00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:36.940
of states parties to the convention

00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:38.830
to evaluate facts and evidence

00:27:38.830 --> 00:27:41.120
on the application of national law,

00:27:41.120 --> 00:27:43.990
unless it can be established
that the evaluation

00:27:43.990 --> 00:27:46.660
was biased or based on gender stereotypes

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:49.800
that constitute
discrimination against women

00:27:49.800 --> 00:27:53.620
was clearly arbitrary or
amounted to a denial of justice.

00:27:53.620 --> 00:27:55.560
So when it's looking at how

00:27:55.560 --> 00:27:58.380
states determine asylum applications,

00:27:58.380 --> 00:28:01.330
when, how the state
approached our essays case,

00:28:01.330 --> 00:28:02.760
this is the standard of review

00:28:02.760 --> 00:28:04.410
that the treaty body is applying.

00:28:05.883 --> 00:28:09.034
So the CEDAW committee looked at the facts

00:28:09.034 --> 00:28:11.730
in her asylum claim and
in particular they looked

00:28:11.730 --> 00:28:14.520
at the way that she has
been deemed incredible.

00:28:14.520 --> 00:28:16.580
They also looked at
the structural barriers

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:20.090
that she faced as a Jordanian individual,

00:28:20.090 --> 00:28:22.880
but somebody who was
old Palestinian origin.

00:28:22.880 --> 00:28:26.359
So someone who was a
refugee and had that history

00:28:26.359 --> 00:28:28.810
in the country where that she experienced

00:28:28.810 --> 00:28:30.730
gender based violence.

00:28:30.730 --> 00:28:32.850
The committee held that she faced

00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:35.870
a real personal and foreseeable risk

00:28:35.870 --> 00:28:38.460
that as a woman who had knowingly
fled her violent husband

00:28:38.460 --> 00:28:41.570
and had fled Jordan with
her two minor daughters

00:28:41.570 --> 00:28:44.880
and was at risk of further
gender-based violence.

00:28:44.880 --> 00:28:47.660
And the committee took an
intersectional approach

00:28:47.660 --> 00:28:51.760
to the discrimination that
she had faced in Jordan.

00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:55.160
And they found that Denmark
hadn't accorded due weight

00:28:55.160 --> 00:28:58.570
to her status as a Palestinian refugee.

00:28:58.570 --> 00:29:02.970
Now, earlier on, I talked about
general recommendation 32,

00:29:02.970 --> 00:29:05.980
and I talked about the standards
that the committee has set

00:29:05.980 --> 00:29:09.690
for how states should
determine asylum applications.

00:29:09.690 --> 00:29:12.460
And when it was applying
the standard of review

00:29:12.460 --> 00:29:16.490
to RSAA's case, the committee
found a number of ways

00:29:16.490 --> 00:29:18.360
in which Denmark had not properly

00:29:18.360 --> 00:29:21.063
applied general recommendation 32.

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:23.410
Essentially, Denmark had failed

00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:24.810
to meet its obligations under CEDAW

00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:29.810
and the return of RSS
sorry, RSAA and her children

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:31.743
would breach the convention.

00:29:32.694 --> 00:29:34.910
So what we have here is
an example of somebody

00:29:34.910 --> 00:29:37.240
who makes a claim for
international protection,

00:29:37.240 --> 00:29:41.370
who argues that they are a
refugee under international law,

00:29:41.370 --> 00:29:45.780
but whose claim is not recognized
by the state that she's in

00:29:45.780 --> 00:29:49.370
and who relies therefore on
CEDAW and the CEDAW committee

00:29:49.370 --> 00:29:53.200
to prevent her deportation,
to prevent her removal.

00:29:53.200 --> 00:29:55.840
And because her case involves
gender based violence

00:29:55.840 --> 00:29:59.260
and because her case
requires the monitoring body

00:29:59.260 --> 00:30:01.060
to take an intersectional approach

00:30:01.060 --> 00:30:03.150
to the violence and
discrimination that she fears,

00:30:03.150 --> 00:30:06.060
she goes to the CEDAW
committee, which as, I hopefully

00:30:06.060 --> 00:30:09.405
have explained to you in the
introduction to this talk

00:30:09.405 --> 00:30:11.930
protects women who are refugees,

00:30:11.930 --> 00:30:13.840
and doesn't discriminate
between the rights

00:30:13.840 --> 00:30:16.230
that nationals and
non-nationals should have,

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:19.690
so should be inappropriate for,
for this kind of complaint.

00:30:19.690 --> 00:30:21.400
And she's successful.

00:30:21.400 --> 00:30:23.450
Denmark cannot remove her.

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:24.720
We don't know if she's recognized

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:27.080
as a refugee as a
consequence of this decision,

00:30:27.080 --> 00:30:28.740
but we know that she's not going to face,

00:30:28.740 --> 00:30:30.450
or we hope that she's not going to face

00:30:30.450 --> 00:30:32.833
deportation to Jordan.

00:30:33.900 --> 00:30:35.700
Now, I want to contrast that case

00:30:35.700 --> 00:30:39.310
with one that was decided
just a few months later,

00:30:39.310 --> 00:30:42.140
and it also concerned
a case against Denmark,

00:30:42.140 --> 00:30:45.663
and it concerned a very
similar factual situation.

00:30:46.600 --> 00:30:50.580
So this time, KIA was
someone who had been resident

00:30:50.580 --> 00:30:53.020
in Denmark for her childhood
and she was tricked

00:30:53.020 --> 00:30:56.793
into leaving the country
and forced into marriage.

00:30:57.920 --> 00:30:59.460
She was subjected to domestic

00:30:59.460 --> 00:31:02.700
and sexual violence for 11 years,

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:05.370
and she eventually managed to escape

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:07.780
and to return to Denmark,

00:31:07.780 --> 00:31:11.200
where she made a claim
for refugee protection.

00:31:11.200 --> 00:31:13.300
And again, her history as someone

00:31:13.300 --> 00:31:17.850
who had Palestinian origins
and the intersectional nature

00:31:17.850 --> 00:31:19.270
of the violence and discrimination

00:31:19.270 --> 00:31:22.453
that she experienced in Jordan
were key parts of her claim.

00:31:23.610 --> 00:31:26.700
But the committee didn't reach
the same decision in her case

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:30.060
that it reached in the
previous one, I discussed.

00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:33.230
They found her communication inadmissible,

00:31:33.230 --> 00:31:36.010
and what they did was
collapse the admissibility

00:31:36.010 --> 00:31:37.680
and merits reasoning.

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:39.840
They applied to her and to her case,

00:31:39.840 --> 00:31:43.320
a much higher standard of review.

00:31:43.320 --> 00:31:45.560
Her account was unsubstantiated

00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:48.550
because she couldn't show
that her treatment was biased

00:31:48.550 --> 00:31:51.463
or based on gender
stereotypes or arbitrary.

00:31:52.660 --> 00:31:55.100
So what these two cases show us

00:31:55.100 --> 00:31:56.920
is that the committee approached

00:31:56.920 --> 00:31:59.930
the situation confronted by two women

00:31:59.930 --> 00:32:03.480
who both faced quite similar
and disturbing levels

00:32:03.480 --> 00:32:05.230
of domestic and sexual violence,

00:32:05.230 --> 00:32:08.910
but in two substantively different ways.

00:32:08.910 --> 00:32:10.430
And in the second situation,

00:32:10.430 --> 00:32:13.020
the committee collapses
two distinct questions,

00:32:13.020 --> 00:32:16.020
the question of admissibility
and the question of whether

00:32:16.020 --> 00:32:18.520
a right had been violated in such a way

00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:23.000
as to preclude the merits
being properly considered.

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:25.801
And what that meant was that whilst RSS,

00:32:25.801 --> 00:32:29.620
RSAA and her children were
protected from deportation,

00:32:29.620 --> 00:32:32.480
KIA and her children were not.

00:32:32.480 --> 00:32:34.770
So having exhausted the asylum

00:32:34.770 --> 00:32:38.320
determination process in
Denmark, they risked refoulement

00:32:39.220 --> 00:32:41.220
potentially to gender-based violence

00:32:41.220 --> 00:32:43.860
and other forms of serious harm.

00:32:43.860 --> 00:32:46.710
They'd gone to an international soft court

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:48.670
to have their rights vindicated,

00:32:48.670 --> 00:32:52.903
and instead those rights
were not properly considered.

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:58.630
So that's the first failure
that I want to highlight about

00:32:58.630 --> 00:33:02.160
the way that the CEDAW
committee approaches

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:04.060
some of the cases that come before it.

00:33:05.030 --> 00:33:09.020
I want to use two more cases
now to show another failure.

00:33:09.020 --> 00:33:11.087
And this is similar to
the first point I made,

00:33:11.087 --> 00:33:12.980
but it is analytically distinct.

00:33:12.980 --> 00:33:15.230
It concerns the standard of review again,

00:33:15.230 --> 00:33:18.290
and it concerns how the
CEDAW committee approaches

00:33:18.290 --> 00:33:22.060
migrants women's rights,
not in the abstract

00:33:22.060 --> 00:33:24.590
when it's looking at
general recommendations,

00:33:24.590 --> 00:33:27.343
but when confronted
with alleged violations.

00:33:28.620 --> 00:33:32.950
So I have two cases that I
want to discuss with you,

00:33:32.950 --> 00:33:36.980
and I want to do these
cases to chart the contours

00:33:36.980 --> 00:33:40.543
of a particular kind of
responsibility deficit.

00:33:41.890 --> 00:33:46.100
So the first case is from a
few years ago now, from 2013,

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:49.890
it concerned a woman who fled Burundi

00:33:49.890 --> 00:33:51.880
and claimed refugee protection

00:33:51.880 --> 00:33:54.690
on the grounds of political persecution.

00:33:54.690 --> 00:33:57.120
She was a member of an opposition party

00:33:57.120 --> 00:33:59.580
and had been involved
as a political activist

00:33:59.580 --> 00:34:02.450
in a number of events and demonstrations.

00:34:02.450 --> 00:34:05.210
Now, while she was attempting
to escape political violence,

00:34:05.210 --> 00:34:08.743
she was raped by more than one
perpetrator at knife point.

00:34:09.590 --> 00:34:11.560
She went through the asylum process

00:34:11.560 --> 00:34:14.073
and her claim was rejected.

00:34:15.030 --> 00:34:17.600
Now she then proceeds to take her case

00:34:17.600 --> 00:34:20.640
to the CEDAW committee to
argue that she should not be

00:34:20.640 --> 00:34:22.840
returned to Burundi.

00:34:22.840 --> 00:34:25.410
And her case is found to be inadmissible,

00:34:25.410 --> 00:34:27.963
but for a very striking reason.

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:31.730
So MEN disclosed the sexual violence

00:34:31.730 --> 00:34:33.940
that she had been subjected,

00:34:33.940 --> 00:34:36.630
but she didn't describe that violence

00:34:36.630 --> 00:34:40.360
in her asylum interview
as being discrimination.

00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:42.540
Instead, she believed
that it was coincidental

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:44.350
that she had been raped.

00:34:44.350 --> 00:34:45.960
Now the CEDAW committee said that

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:49.530
because she phrased that part
of her client in that way,

00:34:49.530 --> 00:34:52.100
the state party, in this case, Denmark

00:34:52.100 --> 00:34:55.160
didn't have the opportunity
to consider her allegations

00:34:55.160 --> 00:34:56.703
of gender based violence.

00:34:57.700 --> 00:35:00.810
Now, this is a really astonishing decision

00:35:00.810 --> 00:35:03.840
for the committee to reach,
because what they're saying is,

00:35:03.840 --> 00:35:07.357
and they say it very clearly
on the face of this case,

00:35:07.357 --> 00:35:09.880
that describing, having been raped

00:35:09.880 --> 00:35:11.730
at knife point by three men,

00:35:11.730 --> 00:35:14.860
but not defining that
treatment as sex discrimination

00:35:14.860 --> 00:35:18.210
is not sufficient to raise that treatment

00:35:18.210 --> 00:35:20.907
in your asylum claim.

00:35:20.907 --> 00:35:22.620
Her case was inadmissible,

00:35:22.620 --> 00:35:25.683
and we don't know whether or
not she was subject to return.

00:35:26.540 --> 00:35:28.480
This case is interesting
because it has a really

00:35:28.480 --> 00:35:30.800
strong dissenting opinion.

00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:32.420
And that dissenting opinion was written

00:35:32.420 --> 00:35:35.220
by someone called Dubravka Simonovic

00:35:35.220 --> 00:35:38.210
who went on to become
the UN Special Rapporteur

00:35:38.210 --> 00:35:42.430
against violence and women,
its causes and consequences.

00:35:42.430 --> 00:35:45.090
And she rooted her dissenting opinion

00:35:45.090 --> 00:35:48.870
in general recommendation,
19 on gender based violence,

00:35:48.870 --> 00:35:51.837
arguing that it was a matter
of customary international law

00:35:51.837 --> 00:35:53.310
and international criminal law,

00:35:53.310 --> 00:35:55.700
as well as the CEDAW committee's own work

00:35:55.700 --> 00:35:58.690
that such sexual violence was
discrimination against women

00:35:58.690 --> 00:36:01.393
and should have been considered
properly in the case.

00:36:02.420 --> 00:36:04.620
So MEN is one example of the committee

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:07.130
failing to apply its own standards

00:36:07.130 --> 00:36:12.130
in a really clear and concrete,
and easy to identify way.

00:36:12.230 --> 00:36:14.610
But I want to draw on a more recent case

00:36:14.610 --> 00:36:17.140
to make this point as well.

00:36:17.140 --> 00:36:19.520
And because I'm talking
to you from Europe,

00:36:19.520 --> 00:36:23.240
I want to use this case to
talk about some of the issues

00:36:23.240 --> 00:36:25.810
that are being confronted
by refugees in Europe,

00:36:25.810 --> 00:36:28.590
at the moment, and in particular,

00:36:28.590 --> 00:36:33.100
their return from one
European country to another.

00:36:33.100 --> 00:36:35.590
Where they've claimed
asylum in one country,

00:36:35.590 --> 00:36:37.883
but traveled within the European Union.

00:36:39.330 --> 00:36:42.550
So at the moment within
the European Union,

00:36:42.550 --> 00:36:46.020
there is a system whereby an individual

00:36:46.020 --> 00:36:47.530
who claims asylum in one country,

00:36:47.530 --> 00:36:49.720
but then travels on
and seeks international

00:36:49.720 --> 00:36:51.900
or other protection in another,

00:36:51.900 --> 00:36:55.000
if they are identified as having
done that will be returned

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:56.570
under European Union law.

00:36:56.570 --> 00:36:59.190
And there are very few
exceptions to this return.

00:36:59.190 --> 00:37:02.390
There are some that may be
based on a family reunion,

00:37:02.390 --> 00:37:05.140
but in general, this way of

00:37:05.140 --> 00:37:06.640
it's called responsibility sharing

00:37:06.640 --> 00:37:08.290
but it's certainly not that,

00:37:08.290 --> 00:37:11.610
is a way of containing
refugees in the first country

00:37:11.610 --> 00:37:15.880
that they claim asylum and
has a disproportionate impact

00:37:15.880 --> 00:37:18.970
on countries like Italy
for example, and Spain

00:37:18.970 --> 00:37:21.470
who receive large numbers of individuals

00:37:21.470 --> 00:37:22.900
seeking international protection

00:37:22.900 --> 00:37:24.400
from across the Mediterranean.

00:37:25.392 --> 00:37:28.900
So Abdi Osman was someone who

00:37:28.900 --> 00:37:33.900
fled truly horrific torture
and violence in Somalia.

00:37:34.340 --> 00:37:37.200
She was kidnapped by a
member of Al-Shabaab,

00:37:37.200 --> 00:37:40.180
she was forced to marry the
person who kidnapped her,

00:37:40.180 --> 00:37:41.780
her father tried to protect her,

00:37:41.780 --> 00:37:43.680
and he was murdered in that incident.

00:37:43.680 --> 00:37:46.350
She was subjected to
torture and sexual violence

00:37:46.350 --> 00:37:48.310
over a number of years.

00:37:48.310 --> 00:37:53.310
She became pregnant three times
and two of those pregnancies

00:37:53.740 --> 00:37:55.660
were ended in a forced abortion

00:37:55.660 --> 00:37:59.680
and the third child who was
born was taken from her.

00:37:59.680 --> 00:38:02.840
She managed to escape
and made the incredibly

00:38:02.840 --> 00:38:06.530
difficult, dangerous, and
illegalized journey to Italy,

00:38:06.530 --> 00:38:09.631
where she was granted
subsidiary protection.

00:38:09.631 --> 00:38:12.490
Now in Italy, the
treatment that she received

00:38:12.490 --> 00:38:13.910
did not meet the standards

00:38:13.910 --> 00:38:16.910
set out in general recommendation 32.

00:38:16.910 --> 00:38:20.570
Ms. Abdi Osman was homeless
for a number of years,

00:38:20.570 --> 00:38:23.500
she was detained, she
wasn't offered protections

00:38:23.500 --> 00:38:27.240
as a survivor of sexual violence
or a survivor of torture.

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:28.290
And she certainly wasn't offered

00:38:28.290 --> 00:38:31.410
any kind of psychosocial
support or assistance.

00:38:31.410 --> 00:38:33.120
And she claimed that she should have been

00:38:33.120 --> 00:38:34.960
recognized as a refugee,

00:38:34.960 --> 00:38:37.500
but instead she was granted
a lesser form of protection,

00:38:37.500 --> 00:38:39.943
complimentary protection
in the European Union.

00:38:41.660 --> 00:38:45.770
She formed a relationship
and she wished to move

00:38:45.770 --> 00:38:48.350
and join her husband in Switzerland,

00:38:48.350 --> 00:38:50.950
another country in the European Union.

00:38:50.950 --> 00:38:54.040
She did so, and she made
a claim for asylum there.

00:38:54.040 --> 00:38:55.940
That claim was refused,

00:38:55.940 --> 00:38:58.530
and she was returned under
the Dublin three regulation,

00:38:58.530 --> 00:39:00.100
the system where individuals are returned

00:39:00.100 --> 00:39:03.810
to the first country they
claimed and to Italy.

00:39:03.810 --> 00:39:06.310
And when we talk about
returns and deportations,

00:39:06.310 --> 00:39:09.010
and that language often
sanitize processes,

00:39:09.010 --> 00:39:11.219
which are incredibly violent

00:39:11.219 --> 00:39:14.050
and harmful to the individuals concerned.

00:39:14.050 --> 00:39:16.140
And we might be familiar with
the violence of deportation

00:39:16.140 --> 00:39:17.770
in the sense of individuals
being handcuffed

00:39:17.770 --> 00:39:20.640
and compelled into immigration
detention or on a plane.

00:39:20.640 --> 00:39:22.430
This was a different type of violence

00:39:22.430 --> 00:39:25.430
because Ms. Abdi Osman
was driven to the border,

00:39:25.430 --> 00:39:28.100
she was given a small
amount of euros in cash,

00:39:28.100 --> 00:39:30.140
and she was left in a car park.

00:39:30.140 --> 00:39:32.440
And she was that in that car park

00:39:32.440 --> 00:39:34.400
with another group of refugees

00:39:34.400 --> 00:39:36.860
and she lived on the streets
for a period of days.

00:39:36.860 --> 00:39:39.770
She wasn't told where to go,
she wasn't told what to do,

00:39:39.770 --> 00:39:42.070
she wasn't given any type of support

00:39:42.070 --> 00:39:44.073
or assistance by the state.

00:39:45.429 --> 00:39:49.650
She slowly made her way back
to her husband to Switzerland,

00:39:49.650 --> 00:39:51.370
and she became pregnant again

00:39:51.370 --> 00:39:54.830
and again, she faces return to Italy.

00:39:54.830 --> 00:39:56.310
Her health, by this point had deteriorated

00:39:56.310 --> 00:39:58.150
quite significantly.

00:39:58.150 --> 00:40:00.160
She brings a claim to the CEDAW committee

00:40:00.160 --> 00:40:03.160
to try and opt out of this European system

00:40:03.160 --> 00:40:06.650
of compelling people to return
from one country to another.

00:40:06.650 --> 00:40:08.803
And she does so on the basis that

00:40:08.803 --> 00:40:11.610
she has been subject to
discrimination and violence,

00:40:11.610 --> 00:40:14.940
not only in her country of
origin, but also in Italy

00:40:14.940 --> 00:40:17.903
where she has been mistreated
now on two occasions.

00:40:19.280 --> 00:40:22.810
The CEDAW committee have
the general recommendation

00:40:22.810 --> 00:40:25.630
that I talk to you
through in front of them.

00:40:25.630 --> 00:40:27.880
And Ms. Abdi Osman's legal representative

00:40:27.880 --> 00:40:31.500
takes them through all of
the rights and protections

00:40:31.500 --> 00:40:33.050
that I described to you.

00:40:33.050 --> 00:40:34.280
And in particular, the support

00:40:34.280 --> 00:40:36.473
that should be accorded
to sexual violence.

00:40:38.510 --> 00:40:41.550
Unaccountably the
committee however, stated

00:40:41.550 --> 00:40:44.040
it is for the sovereign
state party to determine

00:40:44.040 --> 00:40:46.250
the nature, structure and procedures

00:40:46.250 --> 00:40:49.010
of its own asylum system.

00:40:49.010 --> 00:40:53.090
Moreover, they said it was
unclear that Ms. Abdi Osman

00:40:53.090 --> 00:40:55.610
would face no specific indications

00:40:55.610 --> 00:40:59.480
that she would not be
afforded protection in Italy.

00:40:59.480 --> 00:41:00.860
And it reached that decision

00:41:00.860 --> 00:41:03.130
despite the fact that as
I've explained to you,

00:41:03.130 --> 00:41:05.720
she didn't get the
protection or health care

00:41:05.720 --> 00:41:08.730
that she was entitled to
under the CEDAW convention

00:41:08.730 --> 00:41:11.150
on not one but two occasions.

00:41:11.150 --> 00:41:13.270
So she can point to two
examples in the past

00:41:13.270 --> 00:41:14.880
where her rights have been protected

00:41:14.880 --> 00:41:17.600
and even doing so is not sufficient

00:41:17.600 --> 00:41:19.733
to offer her protection from return.

00:41:20.920 --> 00:41:23.240
Her case was denied.

00:41:23.240 --> 00:41:26.520
So this is another example
of the CEDAW committee,

00:41:26.520 --> 00:41:29.320
just not applying, not enforcing

00:41:29.320 --> 00:41:33.040
the standards that it is
identified as being necessary

00:41:33.040 --> 00:41:37.410
to protect refugee women
from discrimination.

00:41:37.410 --> 00:41:40.520
And from a legal perspective,
this is really problematic

00:41:40.520 --> 00:41:43.270
because the committee routes the general

00:41:43.270 --> 00:41:45.557
recommendation, 32 obligations

00:41:45.557 --> 00:41:48.643
in that definition of
discrimination against women

00:41:48.643 --> 00:41:51.460
that I started this seminar with.

00:41:51.460 --> 00:41:53.390
So this isn't presented as being,

00:41:53.390 --> 00:41:55.010
an optional set of protections

00:41:55.010 --> 00:41:57.960
that individuals may be entitled to,

00:41:57.960 --> 00:41:59.230
the CEDAW committee is saying that

00:41:59.230 --> 00:42:03.530
this is required by states as
a matter of international law.

00:42:03.530 --> 00:42:06.940
And yet when women seek to
rely on those standards,

00:42:06.940 --> 00:42:10.730
the CEDAW committee
does not vindicate them.

00:42:10.730 --> 00:42:13.940
And what the decision in
Ms. Abdi Osman's case does

00:42:13.940 --> 00:42:15.910
is leave a woman who has a significant

00:42:15.910 --> 00:42:16.810
set of health problems

00:42:16.810 --> 00:42:19.090
as a result of torture
and sexual violence,

00:42:19.090 --> 00:42:22.340
to be deported with a
newborn baby, to a country,

00:42:22.340 --> 00:42:25.240
which has repeatedly failed
to meet the standards

00:42:25.240 --> 00:42:26.563
the committee has set.

00:42:27.700 --> 00:42:30.360
Now, these two views that I've
given you a not anomalous,

00:42:30.360 --> 00:42:33.480
but representative of others
in which the committee's

00:42:33.480 --> 00:42:35.650
adoption of a general recommendation

00:42:35.650 --> 00:42:38.180
makes little discernible difference

00:42:38.180 --> 00:42:40.520
to its adjudication of communications

00:42:40.520 --> 00:42:41.670
that are brought to it.

00:42:43.280 --> 00:42:45.330
So I'm sure you'll be very pleased to know

00:42:45.330 --> 00:42:48.173
that I'm reaching the
conclusions part of this paper.

00:42:49.620 --> 00:42:51.930
I think, and as I hoped in the first

00:42:51.930 --> 00:42:53.340
couple of slides in this presentation

00:42:53.340 --> 00:42:55.410
to show you that CEDAW is well-placed

00:42:55.410 --> 00:42:58.100
to respond to discrimination
against women,

00:42:58.100 --> 00:43:01.860
that's experienced in the
context of migration control

00:43:01.860 --> 00:43:04.008
and there are a number
of reasons for this.

00:43:04.008 --> 00:43:06.910
Definition of discrimination is capacious.

00:43:06.910 --> 00:43:08.920
As I said, it covers both direct

00:43:08.920 --> 00:43:10.970
and indirect discrimination,

00:43:10.970 --> 00:43:12.699
including that, which is violent,

00:43:12.699 --> 00:43:16.033
whether experienced in the
public or private domain.

00:43:17.260 --> 00:43:18.850
The committee takes a broad effects

00:43:18.850 --> 00:43:21.880
based approach to jurisdiction
and its beneficiaries,

00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:25.330
which enables it in theory
to hold states responsible

00:43:25.330 --> 00:43:29.370
for that extra territorial
and discriminatory conduct.

00:43:29.370 --> 00:43:31.000
And in its general recommendations,

00:43:31.000 --> 00:43:32.700
the committee has capitalized

00:43:32.700 --> 00:43:35.310
on this advantageous positioning,

00:43:35.310 --> 00:43:37.680
interpreting the convention robustly

00:43:37.680 --> 00:43:39.610
and impugning as discriminatory

00:43:39.610 --> 00:43:42.460
a range of laws and practices.

00:43:42.460 --> 00:43:46.770
It's also, as I hope to have
highlighted required states

00:43:46.770 --> 00:43:50.240
to make gender sensitive
determination procedures

00:43:50.240 --> 00:43:53.160
that apply gender
sensitive interpretations

00:43:53.160 --> 00:43:56.400
of core facets of the
international protection regime,

00:43:56.400 --> 00:44:00.460
such as article 1.A (2)
definition of a refugee.

00:44:00.460 --> 00:44:03.880
And more importantly, it
enables or it urges states

00:44:03.880 --> 00:44:06.770
to increase women's
ability to migrate safely

00:44:06.770 --> 00:44:08.893
and access protection lawfully.

00:44:10.075 --> 00:44:13.160
There are however significant
flaws in the reasoning

00:44:13.160 --> 00:44:16.383
that the committee has employed
in a number of instances.

00:44:17.770 --> 00:44:20.370
And I focused in its adjudication

00:44:21.380 --> 00:44:23.880
elements on non-refoulement.

00:44:23.880 --> 00:44:26.220
And the protection that should be accorded

00:44:26.220 --> 00:44:29.173
to women who claim refugee protection.

00:44:30.430 --> 00:44:33.950
It's of concern that so few cases

00:44:33.950 --> 00:44:35.080
are brought to the committee,

00:44:35.080 --> 00:44:37.560
alleging discrimination
and migration control

00:44:37.560 --> 00:44:38.900
when the committee has engaged

00:44:38.900 --> 00:44:41.373
in such standard setting in this area.

00:44:42.280 --> 00:44:44.410
It's of concern that a number of states

00:44:44.410 --> 00:44:47.802
who have the laws and practices
that the committee impugns

00:44:47.802 --> 00:44:50.540
are not being challenged
before the CEDAW committee

00:44:50.540 --> 00:44:52.253
by individuals affected by them.

00:44:53.821 --> 00:44:56.640
When communications are brought,

00:44:56.640 --> 00:44:58.750
the committee's reasoning is disappointing

00:44:58.750 --> 00:45:01.830
and frequently unprincipled.

00:45:01.830 --> 00:45:03.700
And this is the case,

00:45:03.700 --> 00:45:06.263
not withstanding the fact
that in some of its decisions,

00:45:06.263 --> 00:45:10.560
the committee has given
adjudicative expression

00:45:10.560 --> 00:45:13.600
to its commitment, to respond
to multiple and intersecting

00:45:13.600 --> 00:45:15.940
forms of discrimination and inequality.

00:45:15.940 --> 00:45:17.890
And I think the RSSA
case I mentioned before,

00:45:17.890 --> 00:45:19.486
is an example of that.

00:45:19.486 --> 00:45:20.827
It wasn't just the case
that she was a woman

00:45:20.827 --> 00:45:23.610
who was at risk of gender discrimination,

00:45:23.610 --> 00:45:26.700
she was also unable to access
equal protection of the law

00:45:26.700 --> 00:45:28.720
because she was a Palestinian refugee.

00:45:28.720 --> 00:45:32.250
So that's an intersectional
approach being taken to the

00:45:32.250 --> 00:45:35.023
violence and the lack of
protection available to her.

00:45:36.370 --> 00:45:38.270
However, there are a number of other cases

00:45:38.270 --> 00:45:40.230
in the dataset that I examined,

00:45:40.230 --> 00:45:43.870
where the committee takes an
overly deferential approach

00:45:43.870 --> 00:45:46.920
to questions around state power

00:45:46.920 --> 00:45:50.140
and states migration control functions.

00:45:50.140 --> 00:45:52.420
And this deferential
approach seems to have

00:45:52.420 --> 00:45:55.200
two elements to it, either a failure

00:45:55.200 --> 00:45:58.220
to differentiate between
admissibility and merits

00:45:58.220 --> 00:46:02.600
or a failure to apply its own
legal and normative standards.

00:46:02.600 --> 00:46:04.390
And in both of these examples,

00:46:04.390 --> 00:46:06.300
women complaining of
discriminatory treatment

00:46:06.300 --> 00:46:08.553
are exposed to the risk of refoulement.

00:46:09.410 --> 00:46:11.773
So there's a wealth of research
for the CEDAW committee.

00:46:11.773 --> 00:46:13.880
So Loveday Hodson, for example, has said,

00:46:13.880 --> 00:46:15.740
the committee has been unable to establish

00:46:15.740 --> 00:46:18.590
much of a voice on the asylum
claims of vulnerable women.

00:46:19.710 --> 00:46:21.880
Alternatively Bassat
Cali, Katherine Costello

00:46:21.880 --> 00:46:23.370
and Stuart Cunningham have said the

00:46:23.370 --> 00:46:27.223
committee has taken a tentative
and deferential approach.

00:46:28.330 --> 00:46:30.720
I hope that you will
have seen by now that my

00:46:30.720 --> 00:46:32.670
conclusions are much more scathing

00:46:32.670 --> 00:46:34.690
of the committee's actions in this regard.

00:46:34.690 --> 00:46:37.090
I think that their
reasoning is unprincipled

00:46:37.090 --> 00:46:38.970
and as a result of that, women's rights

00:46:38.970 --> 00:46:43.970
in the context of refugee
protection go unrealized.

00:46:44.120 --> 00:46:45.830
The core kind of conclusion

00:46:45.830 --> 00:46:47.670
that I am reaching in this research

00:46:47.670 --> 00:46:49.680
is that the committee needs to

00:46:49.680 --> 00:46:52.350
eliminate all forms of discrimination,

00:46:52.350 --> 00:46:54.420
it has to delineate the obligations

00:46:54.420 --> 00:46:58.137
with clarity and precision,
and then it must hold states

00:46:58.137 --> 00:47:02.853
responsible for violating the
standards it has itself set.

00:47:04.310 --> 00:47:06.900
So I appreciate that that was 45 minutes

00:47:06.900 --> 00:47:08.920
that was quite dense
from a legal perspective,

00:47:08.920 --> 00:47:10.380
and that some of you may not be lawyers

00:47:10.380 --> 00:47:12.680
or have a background in international law,

00:47:12.680 --> 00:47:14.280
so if you have any questions at all

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:16.500
about any of the material
that I've covered,

00:47:16.500 --> 00:47:20.150
I'd be delighted to discuss
them with you further.

00:47:20.150 --> 00:47:22.523
- I have a question, can you see me?

00:47:22.523 --> 00:47:23.590
Here.

00:47:23.590 --> 00:47:24.423
- Yes, please do go ahead

00:47:24.423 --> 00:47:25.740
and I can hear you wonderfully too.

00:47:25.740 --> 00:47:26.920
- Okay, great.

00:47:26.920 --> 00:47:31.750
So just thinking really
practically about the US context,

00:47:31.750 --> 00:47:33.440
is the US one of the countries

00:47:33.440 --> 00:47:36.070
that is not a signatory of CEDAW.

00:47:36.070 --> 00:47:39.750
And I'm also thinking about how Trump also

00:47:39.750 --> 00:47:43.460
removed domestic violence as like

00:47:43.460 --> 00:47:45.380
as characteristic of a particular

00:47:45.380 --> 00:47:47.300
kind of group based discrimination.

00:47:47.300 --> 00:47:49.130
I'm just wondering if you can speak

00:47:49.130 --> 00:47:51.660
more to the US context here.

00:47:51.660 --> 00:47:54.230
- Yes, so I...

00:47:54.230 --> 00:47:56.400
There is something called the United

00:47:56.400 --> 00:47:59.100
Nations Treaty Depository, and

00:47:59.100 --> 00:48:00.790
I can share a link to that
in the chat in a moment,

00:48:00.790 --> 00:48:03.080
and you can look up which states

00:48:03.080 --> 00:48:04.970
have signed and ratified CEDAW

00:48:04.970 --> 00:48:08.270
and I'm afraid the US is a
real outlier in this regard

00:48:08.270 --> 00:48:12.370
in being one of the few
states that has not done that.

00:48:12.370 --> 00:48:15.260
Now, as I understand it
under US immigration law,

00:48:15.260 --> 00:48:18.010
individuals can seek
withholding from removal

00:48:18.010 --> 00:48:20.803
on the basis of CAT, the
Convention Against Torture.

00:48:21.640 --> 00:48:23.160
And so the Convention Against Torture

00:48:23.160 --> 00:48:25.110
is very similar to CEDAW

00:48:25.110 --> 00:48:29.330
in that it's a United
Nations legal instrument

00:48:29.330 --> 00:48:33.250
that defines a particular
kind of wrong and then

00:48:33.250 --> 00:48:36.230
protects individuals from
either experiencing that

00:48:36.230 --> 00:48:39.422
in the country that they're
in or being returned to that.

00:48:39.422 --> 00:48:42.040
So the US asylum system
does engage with a very

00:48:42.040 --> 00:48:43.750
limited way with international law,

00:48:43.750 --> 00:48:45.450
with the refugee convention and with CAT

00:48:45.450 --> 00:48:49.010
but it doesn't engage with CEDAW

00:48:49.010 --> 00:48:50.560
in the way that I've described.

00:48:52.950 --> 00:48:55.210
Could you repeat the second
part of your question?

00:48:55.210 --> 00:48:57.890
- Oh, I guess just the other one was that,

00:48:57.890 --> 00:48:59.210
I guess I'm just thinking about

00:48:59.210 --> 00:49:02.370
always the tension
between international law

00:49:02.370 --> 00:49:05.170
and like sovereignty questions, right.

00:49:05.170 --> 00:49:07.130
I'm thinking specifically about

00:49:07.130 --> 00:49:09.420
the Trump administration's decision

00:49:09.420 --> 00:49:12.480
to not count domestic violence as

00:49:12.480 --> 00:49:15.360
like under the definition of
group-based discrimination

00:49:16.820 --> 00:49:20.570
and how that changed,
like the ability for women

00:49:20.570 --> 00:49:22.403
to make claims for asylum.

00:49:23.650 --> 00:49:26.023
And then I'm thinking about like specific,

00:49:27.310 --> 00:49:31.240
some of the rumblings in
the European Union about

00:49:31.240 --> 00:49:33.350
not just reservations to these clauses,

00:49:33.350 --> 00:49:36.160
but the ways in which
they're just flouting them

00:49:36.160 --> 00:49:38.660
entirely under the name,

00:49:38.660 --> 00:49:42.140
under the discourse of
crisis or things like that.

00:49:42.140 --> 00:49:44.790
So I'm just wondering if
you could speak to like,

00:49:44.790 --> 00:49:47.790
the way that maybe some of the deferential

00:49:48.980 --> 00:49:52.410
posturing of CEDAW has to
do with playing this game

00:49:52.410 --> 00:49:55.650
of trying to keep
countries sort of onboard

00:49:55.650 --> 00:49:58.300
on a day to day basis.

00:49:58.300 --> 00:49:59.147
Does that make sense?

00:49:59.147 --> 00:50:02.110
- I'm going to try and
answer each of those in turn

00:50:02.110 --> 00:50:04.652
and do, let me know if
I don't do so fully.

00:50:04.652 --> 00:50:07.440
So the first one was around the specific

00:50:07.440 --> 00:50:11.130
jurisprudence in the US around
particular social groups.

00:50:11.130 --> 00:50:13.850
And I mentioned earlier
that the US was an outlier

00:50:13.850 --> 00:50:16.610
in taking a very limited approach

00:50:16.610 --> 00:50:19.480
to the definition of what
is a particular social group

00:50:19.480 --> 00:50:22.650
and seeking through various means

00:50:22.650 --> 00:50:25.230
to exclude women who are at
risk of domestic violence

00:50:25.230 --> 00:50:28.220
from that definition but also
individuals who are at risk

00:50:28.220 --> 00:50:30.510
of gang related violence as well.

00:50:30.510 --> 00:50:32.730
The common denominators here being

00:50:32.730 --> 00:50:35.472
that it's violence perpetrated
by a non-state actor

00:50:35.472 --> 00:50:37.480
and there are issues around how you define

00:50:37.480 --> 00:50:40.590
the particular social group
in the US is very restrictive

00:50:40.590 --> 00:50:42.240
approach to that question.

00:50:42.240 --> 00:50:43.390
But then there's a second set of questions

00:50:43.390 --> 00:50:45.550
about how you identify whether or not

00:50:45.550 --> 00:50:47.380
the state is willing to protect

00:50:47.380 --> 00:50:49.970
individuals from particular
forms of violence.

00:50:49.970 --> 00:50:51.860
And again, within the US jurisprudence,

00:50:51.860 --> 00:50:54.090
as I understand it,
there has been attempts

00:50:54.090 --> 00:50:57.590
to significantly increase
what an applicant has to do

00:50:57.590 --> 00:51:00.860
to show that the state is
not able to protect them.

00:51:00.860 --> 00:51:02.750
And the reason why I'm
hedging all my comments here

00:51:02.750 --> 00:51:05.610
is that this is very
contested at the moment

00:51:05.610 --> 00:51:08.517
that these cases are being
litigated backwards and forwards.

00:51:08.517 --> 00:51:10.910
And there have been some
quite notable successes

00:51:10.910 --> 00:51:14.670
in pushing back against
some of these approaches.

00:51:14.670 --> 00:51:18.380
Nevertheless, I think the
core kind of consistent strand

00:51:18.380 --> 00:51:22.520
in US jurisprudence is
a failure to recognize

00:51:22.520 --> 00:51:25.080
that women who are fleeing
gender based violence

00:51:26.099 --> 00:51:29.140
are refugees both on the member

00:51:29.140 --> 00:51:30.590
of a particular social group ground,

00:51:30.590 --> 00:51:31.750
but also really importantly,

00:51:31.750 --> 00:51:34.270
potentially on the political
opinion ground as well.

00:51:34.270 --> 00:51:36.390
I've come across one case at which

00:51:36.390 --> 00:51:39.100
some wonderful students of
mine from last year shared,

00:51:39.100 --> 00:51:42.630
which was a US case where
resistance to domestic violence,

00:51:42.630 --> 00:51:44.050
feminist resistance to domestic violence

00:51:44.050 --> 00:51:46.003
was interpreted as a political opinion.

00:51:47.180 --> 00:51:49.500
And that's something that has been

00:51:49.500 --> 00:51:52.500
litigated quite successfully
in other jurisdictions,

00:51:52.500 --> 00:51:54.460
particularly for example,

00:51:54.460 --> 00:51:56.593
in New Zealand and also within the EU.

00:51:58.950 --> 00:52:02.619
Now, if the US were to
sign and ratify CEDAW,

00:52:02.619 --> 00:52:05.830
and if it were to use CEDAWE
as an interpretive tool,

00:52:05.830 --> 00:52:10.080
we might expect some improvements
in that jurisprudence.

00:52:10.080 --> 00:52:11.610
But you alluded to a
separate set of problems,

00:52:11.610 --> 00:52:14.150
which are those currently
faced within the European Union

00:52:14.150 --> 00:52:16.310
and Council of Europe at the moment.

00:52:16.310 --> 00:52:18.350
And these don't go just to CEDAW,

00:52:18.350 --> 00:52:20.740
they go to something called
the Istanbul Convention,

00:52:20.740 --> 00:52:22.630
which is the Council of Europe Convention

00:52:22.630 --> 00:52:25.350
on Violence and
Discrimination Against Women.

00:52:25.350 --> 00:52:27.710
And this convention is really important

00:52:27.710 --> 00:52:30.030
because it embeds a lot
of what CEDAW has done

00:52:30.030 --> 00:52:31.690
within the council of Europe.

00:52:31.690 --> 00:52:34.720
But it also has specific articles in it

00:52:34.720 --> 00:52:37.540
which protect migrant and refugee women,

00:52:37.540 --> 00:52:40.311
including by offering
them migration statuses

00:52:40.311 --> 00:52:43.000
and by ensuring some of the
things that I've talked about

00:52:43.000 --> 00:52:46.560
in this talk are incorporated
into states national law,

00:52:46.560 --> 00:52:48.350
like gender sensitive interpretations

00:52:48.350 --> 00:52:49.653
of the refugee convention.

00:52:51.210 --> 00:52:53.250
Now a number of states have attempted

00:52:53.250 --> 00:52:55.030
to put reservations into those treaties

00:52:55.030 --> 00:52:57.280
or have unsigned them.

00:52:57.280 --> 00:53:00.750
Or states have taken quite a

00:53:03.290 --> 00:53:05.050
negative approach to the convention.

00:53:05.050 --> 00:53:06.160
They said a series of things about

00:53:06.160 --> 00:53:07.979
the way that convention protects

00:53:07.979 --> 00:53:10.390
intersectionally on the
grounds of sexual orientation,

00:53:10.390 --> 00:53:12.635
for example, and use that as a pretext

00:53:12.635 --> 00:53:15.760
for not offering protection from violence.

00:53:15.760 --> 00:53:19.786
And I think this needs to
be seen within a broader

00:53:19.786 --> 00:53:22.930
response to authoritarianism

00:53:22.930 --> 00:53:25.340
and not just authoritarianism in Europe,

00:53:25.340 --> 00:53:28.750
but how we think about the
role of international law in

00:53:28.750 --> 00:53:32.270
responding or countering
some of the gendered impacts

00:53:32.270 --> 00:53:35.290
of the rise of authoritarianism
in certain states.

00:53:35.290 --> 00:53:39.280
And that's something
that many women who are

00:53:39.280 --> 00:53:41.640
being activist in Poland
at the moment around

00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:43.820
changes to the law and
abortion, for example,

00:53:43.820 --> 00:53:46.060
are having to contest on the streets

00:53:46.060 --> 00:53:48.413
while we talk about it in this classroom.

00:53:51.587 --> 00:53:52.420
- Thank you.

00:53:54.119 --> 00:53:58.450
- I have some quite a few questions.

00:53:58.450 --> 00:54:00.653
So one is that, for example,

00:54:04.250 --> 00:54:07.420
is there once the CEDAW has given

00:54:09.028 --> 00:54:10.170
its determination,

00:54:10.170 --> 00:54:15.170
is there a way to appeal
then the other one is that,

00:54:18.230 --> 00:54:20.330
I mean, given the difference

00:54:20.330 --> 00:54:23.483
that they have been
reaching some of the cases,

00:54:24.690 --> 00:54:29.690
could that be explained,
for example, in the initial

00:54:29.970 --> 00:54:33.330
representation that
these women have had in

00:54:33.330 --> 00:54:37.250
maybe like access initially to like

00:54:38.385 --> 00:54:41.990
a lawyer or two, or that
they are aware of how

00:54:41.990 --> 00:54:44.710
they can bring the case.

00:54:44.710 --> 00:54:48.420
And then the other question
that I have is that,

00:54:48.420 --> 00:54:51.060
how is this committee

00:54:51.920 --> 00:54:53.930
form and then for how long,

00:54:53.930 --> 00:54:58.780
I mean, you said that some of the people

00:55:00.050 --> 00:55:03.130
that are part of the committee
basically have some views,

00:55:03.130 --> 00:55:07.323
or they have evolved over time as well.

00:55:08.720 --> 00:55:10.070
- So on appeal, no,

00:55:10.070 --> 00:55:12.740
if the committee declares
a decision inadmissible,

00:55:12.740 --> 00:55:14.307
there's no way to appeal that.

00:55:14.307 --> 00:55:18.090
Though you may be able to
bring a case before another

00:55:18.090 --> 00:55:20.810
international body, another
human rights treaty body,

00:55:20.810 --> 00:55:23.300
for example, like the
committee that oversees

00:55:23.300 --> 00:55:25.590
the convention on the elimination
of race discrimination,

00:55:25.590 --> 00:55:26.990
or before the CAT committee.

00:55:28.420 --> 00:55:31.130
Now you're not allowed to
bring cases before multiple

00:55:32.090 --> 00:55:33.320
human rights treaty bodies,

00:55:33.320 --> 00:55:36.070
because that's precluded by
admissibility requirements,

00:55:36.070 --> 00:55:38.913
but individuals do bring
cases often to more than one.

00:55:39.840 --> 00:55:42.150
I think one of the things
that's interesting to look at

00:55:42.150 --> 00:55:44.870
is why some human rights treaty bodies,

00:55:44.870 --> 00:55:47.950
scrutinize state action, more than others.

00:55:47.950 --> 00:55:50.720
And there is kind of an
emerging strand in the research

00:55:50.720 --> 00:55:54.120
that indicates that the CAT
committee is much more robust

00:55:54.120 --> 00:55:57.270
in protecting individuals
rights when they alleged

00:55:57.270 --> 00:55:59.807
that they run the risk of being refouled.

00:55:59.807 --> 00:56:01.920
And that in comparison,

00:56:01.920 --> 00:56:04.690
the ICCPR, the human rights committee

00:56:04.690 --> 00:56:09.530
and the CEDAW committee take
a much less robust approach.

00:56:09.530 --> 00:56:11.900
And I think one of the things
you might want to think about,

00:56:11.900 --> 00:56:13.310
although perhaps how you would design

00:56:13.310 --> 00:56:14.750
this research is an interesting question

00:56:14.750 --> 00:56:17.260
is why committees, which have quite

00:56:17.260 --> 00:56:19.630
similar standards review,

00:56:19.630 --> 00:56:22.180
why some committees are much
more deferential to states

00:56:22.180 --> 00:56:23.940
than others are.

00:56:23.940 --> 00:56:27.060
I think with CAT, the
Convention Against Torture's

00:56:27.060 --> 00:56:29.030
definition of torture is very narrow

00:56:29.030 --> 00:56:29.930
in comparison with, for example,

00:56:29.930 --> 00:56:32.970
CEDAW's definition of
gender-based violence

00:56:32.970 --> 00:56:36.880
and so it may be that its
precision and its focus,

00:56:36.880 --> 00:56:39.550
and that narrowness
enables the CAT committee

00:56:39.550 --> 00:56:42.720
to take a much more
robust approach to states.

00:56:42.720 --> 00:56:44.900
Whereas because CEDAW
potentially offers much

00:56:44.900 --> 00:56:46.840
broader panoply of rights,

00:56:46.840 --> 00:56:50.911
it's therefore less
assertive and enforcing them.

00:56:50.911 --> 00:56:52.817
I mean, we would have to think about

00:56:52.817 --> 00:56:55.170
whether that was the correct
and principled legal approach,

00:56:55.170 --> 00:56:57.187
but that may be one reason why

00:56:57.187 --> 00:56:59.253
the CEDAW committee is more differential.

00:57:00.340 --> 00:57:02.090
The other thing I wrote
down was membership.

00:57:02.090 --> 00:57:06.030
So yes, members serve for
particular periods of time,

00:57:06.030 --> 00:57:07.750
they are nominated, I cannot remember

00:57:07.750 --> 00:57:08.931
off the top of my head how long.

00:57:08.931 --> 00:57:11.210
I'm going to say five years,
but we can check. (laughs)

00:57:11.210 --> 00:57:12.730
The relevant documents.

00:57:12.730 --> 00:57:14.137
So it will be set out in the convention

00:57:14.137 --> 00:57:16.380
and for how many years.

00:57:16.380 --> 00:57:19.630
Individuals are nominated
by states to serve

00:57:19.630 --> 00:57:21.210
but when you are there,

00:57:21.210 --> 00:57:24.593
you serve in your individual
capacity as an expert.

00:57:25.440 --> 00:57:27.600
So you should not put
forward, for example,

00:57:27.600 --> 00:57:29.580
your state's views on
any particular problem,

00:57:29.580 --> 00:57:31.867
you sit as an independent individual

00:57:31.867 --> 00:57:35.440
who's hopefully either legally
informed or legally rooted,

00:57:35.440 --> 00:57:37.600
or has that background
that would enable you to

00:57:37.600 --> 00:57:40.010
interpret the convention correctly.

00:57:40.010 --> 00:57:43.020
And sorry, I missed the
third part of your question.

00:57:43.020 --> 00:57:43.853
- No.

00:57:43.853 --> 00:57:45.183
So I was thinking if,

00:57:47.016 --> 00:57:47.849
yeah.

00:57:50.620 --> 00:57:54.540
How they're treating the cases
would differ either because

00:57:56.190 --> 00:58:00.220
their own background or
that they're representing

00:58:00.220 --> 00:58:05.220
or because it happens to have
a composition that basically

00:58:06.640 --> 00:58:09.800
is more harsh than others

00:58:11.020 --> 00:58:12.170
in certain cases.

00:58:12.170 --> 00:58:15.310
- So I think that's a really
interesting hypothesis,

00:58:15.310 --> 00:58:17.870
but I don't think that...

00:58:17.870 --> 00:58:20.760
I suspect that that's not the reason

00:58:20.760 --> 00:58:22.770
simply because all human
rights treaty bodies

00:58:22.770 --> 00:58:25.477
have very similar
processes by which experts

00:58:25.477 --> 00:58:28.510
are nominated by states and
then serve on committees.

00:58:28.510 --> 00:58:33.510
- And the fact that, I mean,
how do the women access,

00:58:35.790 --> 00:58:39.530
I mean, help in order to bring
their cases to the CEDAW,

00:58:39.530 --> 00:58:40.980
is that-
- All right, legal aid?

00:58:40.980 --> 00:58:42.180
Yes, you mentioned that.

00:58:43.070 --> 00:58:47.250
So again, that will depend on each state's

00:58:47.250 --> 00:58:48.980
national provisions.

00:58:48.980 --> 00:58:51.320
So within the European
Union, a number of states

00:58:51.320 --> 00:58:52.300
and to the UK,

00:58:52.300 --> 00:58:54.590
obviously you need to remember
the European Union now,

00:58:54.590 --> 00:58:56.880
do have provisions that enable individuals

00:58:56.880 --> 00:58:59.230
to receive legal advice and representation

00:58:59.230 --> 00:59:01.130
that's free of charge.

00:59:01.130 --> 00:59:03.200
And even when that's not available,

00:59:03.200 --> 00:59:05.240
there are a number of
not-for-profit organizations

00:59:05.240 --> 00:59:07.890
who also provide that assistance.

00:59:07.890 --> 00:59:09.017
Interestingly, a number
of the cases I reviewed

00:59:09.017 --> 00:59:11.470
are individuals who bring those cases

00:59:11.470 --> 00:59:13.023
representing themselves.

00:59:13.960 --> 00:59:17.670
And so the quality of legal
advice I think, is a real issue.

00:59:17.670 --> 00:59:19.680
I think you're absolutely
right in pointing to that

00:59:19.680 --> 00:59:21.860
as being one of the
reasons why of these cases

00:59:21.860 --> 00:59:25.920
are not successful when they
should be on their face.

00:59:25.920 --> 00:59:27.151
- Okay.

00:59:27.151 --> 00:59:32.030
And then I imagine that once
you start the process, then,

00:59:32.030 --> 00:59:35.210
I mean, like the case that
you shown that because

00:59:35.210 --> 00:59:39.428
it has not been set in a
specific way so therefore

00:59:39.428 --> 00:59:42.550
it has to set the
consequences as well so...

00:59:42.550 --> 00:59:43.759
- So,

00:59:43.759 --> 00:59:48.040
now that may be a problem within
some courts and tribunals,

00:59:48.040 --> 00:59:50.886
but it shouldn't be before
a human rights treaty bodies

00:59:50.886 --> 00:59:53.210
who don't see individuals.

00:59:53.210 --> 00:59:54.580
So you don't litigate directly.

00:59:54.580 --> 00:59:57.300
You don't argue in Geneva
or in New York, for example,

00:59:57.300 --> 00:59:59.320
you don't stand at the lectern and explain

00:59:59.320 --> 01:00:00.661
how your rights have been violated.

01:00:00.661 --> 01:00:03.050
A lot of these cases are just decided

01:00:03.050 --> 01:00:04.240
on the basis of materials,

01:00:04.240 --> 01:00:05.900
which are submitted to the committee,

01:00:05.900 --> 01:00:08.020
which the committee then reviews.

01:00:08.020 --> 01:00:11.500
And because the committees
procedures around evidence

01:00:11.500 --> 01:00:14.640
and how it makes decisions
are fairly flexible,

01:00:14.640 --> 01:00:17.120
I think you are going to be
kind of less disadvantaged

01:00:17.120 --> 01:00:18.690
as an individual representing yourself

01:00:18.690 --> 01:00:21.030
before human rights
treaty bodies, potentially

01:00:21.030 --> 01:00:23.490
than before, for example,
the Inter-American court

01:00:23.490 --> 01:00:25.763
or the European court of human rights.

01:00:28.710 --> 01:00:29.730
It's interesting as well,

01:00:29.730 --> 01:00:31.430
that in a number of
the cases from Denmark,

01:00:31.430 --> 01:00:34.010
legal representation was
highlighted as being a problem.

01:00:34.010 --> 01:00:37.340
And I think the committee
is rightly concerned

01:00:37.340 --> 01:00:41.000
that cases are just being
brought to the CEDAW committee

01:00:41.000 --> 01:00:42.319
where the individual consent

01:00:42.319 --> 01:00:45.600
is not receiving the
quality of legal advice

01:00:45.600 --> 01:00:47.440
and representation that they should.

01:00:47.440 --> 01:00:48.379
And I think I'll leave there.

01:00:48.379 --> 01:00:51.200
(indistinct)
(laughs)

01:00:51.200 --> 01:00:54.180
- [Trinh] This is a really
specific question, Catherine,

01:00:54.180 --> 01:00:56.683
why do you pick nine-year
for your data sets?

01:00:59.053 --> 01:01:02.490
- Do you know that that's simply
the case that I was looking

01:01:02.490 --> 01:01:06.970
for a range of communications
that would be both before

01:01:06.970 --> 01:01:09.810
and after the adoption of the particular

01:01:09.810 --> 01:01:11.930
general recommendations
that I was interested in.

01:01:11.930 --> 01:01:15.700
So in the last decade, we
have general recommendations

01:01:15.700 --> 01:01:18.580
adopted on trafficking, on
asylum and refugee status

01:01:18.580 --> 01:01:20.080
and gender-based violence.

01:01:20.080 --> 01:01:22.330
And so I wanted a number of communications

01:01:22.330 --> 01:01:24.660
to come from before and after the dates

01:01:24.660 --> 01:01:27.180
that those recommendations
were adopted to see

01:01:27.180 --> 01:01:28.820
what the adoption of a recommendation,

01:01:28.820 --> 01:01:32.050
what difference that
made to particular cases.

01:01:32.050 --> 01:01:34.700
And so nine years just
enabled me to do that

01:01:34.700 --> 01:01:38.340
and enabled me to read the
kind of requisite volume of

01:01:38.340 --> 01:01:40.720
communications, to be
able to reach decisions.

01:01:40.720 --> 01:01:43.320
You could go back
further, fairly obviously.

01:01:43.320 --> 01:01:47.990
And all of the conclusions that
I have started to set out to

01:01:47.990 --> 01:01:50.750
are premised on the fact that
that's a limited dataset,

01:01:50.750 --> 01:01:52.283
nine years worth of cases.

01:01:53.960 --> 01:01:56.750
- Going back, sorry, to some of the point

01:01:56.750 --> 01:01:58.910
that I mentioned, I
mean, do you know there,

01:01:58.910 --> 01:02:01.840
how many times the
committees change as well?

01:02:01.840 --> 01:02:02.900
I mean, there are like...

01:02:02.900 --> 01:02:05.230
If they were to be like five years,

01:02:05.230 --> 01:02:10.153
so sort of see two or three sort of set

01:02:12.626 --> 01:02:13.573
of committee just,

01:02:14.550 --> 01:02:17.930
I mean, just to test the hypothesis that

01:02:17.930 --> 01:02:22.930
some composition might be
sort of harsher to other ones

01:02:23.237 --> 01:02:25.050
of the committee.

01:02:25.050 --> 01:02:26.990
- So I think that that information

01:02:26.990 --> 01:02:28.980
must be publicly available,

01:02:28.980 --> 01:02:31.200
how long people served, when people joined

01:02:31.200 --> 01:02:32.880
and when people left.

01:02:32.880 --> 01:02:34.890
I think the two cases that I showed

01:02:34.890 --> 01:02:39.110
that concerned individuals
who were Palestinian refugees,

01:02:39.110 --> 01:02:40.240
who claimed asylum in Denmark,

01:02:40.240 --> 01:02:42.250
which were decided so close to each other

01:02:42.250 --> 01:02:43.930
indicates that it's not the composition

01:02:43.930 --> 01:02:46.550
of the committee that's
necessarily the problem.

01:02:46.550 --> 01:02:48.778
And because one would imagine that it was

01:02:48.778 --> 01:02:51.300
not necessarily entirely
the same group of people

01:02:51.300 --> 01:02:52.344
sitting on the committee,

01:02:52.344 --> 01:02:53.940
but that there would be
quite a significant overlap

01:02:53.940 --> 01:02:55.320
between the individual sitting.

01:02:55.320 --> 01:02:57.300
- [Teresa] Yeah.
- That's certainly an avenue

01:02:57.300 --> 01:02:58.530
that I could and should pursue.

01:02:58.530 --> 01:03:00.740
So thank you for suggesting it.

01:03:00.740 --> 01:03:01.950
- Any more questions

01:03:03.190 --> 01:03:06.010
in the room or on the

01:03:06.920 --> 01:03:07.763
in Zoom?

01:03:09.040 --> 01:03:10.520
No.

01:03:10.520 --> 01:03:12.218
- I have a question if that's okay.

01:03:12.218 --> 01:03:13.740
(laughs)

01:03:13.740 --> 01:03:15.850
Because I'm aware that I'm predominantly

01:03:15.850 --> 01:03:18.110
potentially speaking to an audience

01:03:18.110 --> 01:03:20.970
who are based in the US or
potentially who are US citizens

01:03:20.970 --> 01:03:24.030
and are familiar with US
immigration and nationality law.

01:03:24.030 --> 01:03:25.743
I will be interested in to what extent

01:03:25.743 --> 01:03:28.810
you find arguments based
on international law,

01:03:28.810 --> 01:03:31.110
interesting or compelling or relevant

01:03:31.110 --> 01:03:33.230
to your research and interests.

01:03:33.230 --> 01:03:35.030
Because if you're within Europe

01:03:35.030 --> 01:03:37.210
kind of geographically
rather than politically,

01:03:37.210 --> 01:03:39.060
these are arguments that
you're very familiar with

01:03:39.060 --> 01:03:40.400
and that you would engage in

01:03:40.400 --> 01:03:43.899
potentially even before
domestic tribunals,

01:03:43.899 --> 01:03:45.760
but I'm aware that that's not the case

01:03:45.760 --> 01:03:47.760
potentially for a number
of you who may be involved

01:03:47.760 --> 01:03:50.070
in activism or litigation or research

01:03:50.070 --> 01:03:53.710
where international law
potentially seems quite removed or

01:03:53.710 --> 01:03:56.160
not relevant to the types of arguments

01:03:56.160 --> 01:03:58.940
that you're interested in making.

01:03:58.940 --> 01:04:00.101
- I could...

01:04:00.101 --> 01:04:03.120
I'm not a legal scholar, but
I'm a migration and border

01:04:03.120 --> 01:04:04.650
enforcement scholar.

01:04:04.650 --> 01:04:07.810
And I think I can speak
first just as a US citizen

01:04:07.810 --> 01:04:10.910
to the way in which I think
international laws still

01:04:10.910 --> 01:04:14.910
like broadly read as like an
overstepping of sovereignty.

01:04:14.910 --> 01:04:17.240
I think that actually probably is the

01:04:17.240 --> 01:04:19.950
sort of sentiment on the street

01:04:19.950 --> 01:04:22.710
and I think that that's
growing in Europe too, right.

01:04:22.710 --> 01:04:24.370
But I'm also...

01:04:24.370 --> 01:04:27.150
But my research takes
place in North Africa

01:04:27.150 --> 01:04:29.150
and in West Africa.

01:04:29.150 --> 01:04:32.770
Where international law is often viewed as

01:04:32.770 --> 01:04:36.750
sort of just an extension
of a colonial imposition

01:04:37.790 --> 01:04:40.330
and particularly around
issues of gender, right?

01:04:40.330 --> 01:04:42.510
Like you have such a different,

01:04:42.510 --> 01:04:46.290
such different interpretations
of what constitutes violence

01:04:47.330 --> 01:04:48.163
sort of,

01:04:50.500 --> 01:04:52.660
all those, like all that bundle of issues

01:04:52.660 --> 01:04:54.443
around gender and then also,

01:04:54.443 --> 01:04:57.160
if we're thinking about
international courts,

01:04:57.160 --> 01:04:58.930
we're thinking about

01:04:58.930 --> 01:05:00.940
who gets prosecuted in those courts.

01:05:00.940 --> 01:05:03.610
They're mostly like post-colonial leaders.

01:05:03.610 --> 01:05:05.800
And so there is a lot of resistance.

01:05:05.800 --> 01:05:08.220
I think there's both at the state level,

01:05:08.220 --> 01:05:10.800
a diplomatic and geopolitical

01:05:12.150 --> 01:05:14.860
motivation to participate
in these conventions,

01:05:14.860 --> 01:05:17.650
but there's also like a deep seated

01:05:17.650 --> 01:05:20.470
suspicion and resistance against them

01:05:22.443 --> 01:05:24.660
to the end that some of
the African countries

01:05:24.660 --> 01:05:26.110
have formed their own court, right.

01:05:26.110 --> 01:05:29.161
Like, so, yeah, so it's
complicated, I think.

01:05:29.161 --> 01:05:30.830
(chuckles)

01:05:30.830 --> 01:05:32.900
- I mean, one of the cases
I excluded from the sample

01:05:32.900 --> 01:05:36.260
was a woman who got recognized
as a refugee in the UK,

01:05:36.260 --> 01:05:37.930
but then brought a case against Libya

01:05:37.930 --> 01:05:40.760
for the gender-based violence
that she had experienced

01:05:40.760 --> 01:05:43.830
there as a feminist activist.

01:05:43.830 --> 01:05:46.710
So I would say that it's
not entirely the case

01:05:46.710 --> 01:05:48.900
that international law is kind of viewed

01:05:48.900 --> 01:05:50.260
with suspicion in those instances,

01:05:50.260 --> 01:05:52.100
because this was an example of somebody

01:05:52.100 --> 01:05:54.140
who, when they got to a
jurisdiction of safety,

01:05:54.140 --> 01:05:56.430
one of the first things they
did was bring a case against

01:05:56.430 --> 01:05:58.100
the country that they had fled from

01:05:58.100 --> 01:06:00.460
to argue that their international

01:06:00.460 --> 01:06:02.660
legally protected rights had been violated

01:06:02.660 --> 01:06:05.400
and interestingly, that case
was entirely successful.

01:06:05.400 --> 01:06:08.480
And so that kind of
speaks to your research,

01:06:08.480 --> 01:06:11.310
but I think it also reflects
on some of the things

01:06:11.310 --> 01:06:12.160
that I've been talking about,

01:06:12.160 --> 01:06:14.410
which is when somebody is
recognized as a refugee

01:06:14.410 --> 01:06:16.270
and they're bringing
a case about the state

01:06:16.270 --> 01:06:17.610
that they have fled from,

01:06:17.610 --> 01:06:19.610
the CEDAW committee can recognize that

01:06:19.610 --> 01:06:21.370
and find the violations.

01:06:21.370 --> 01:06:24.090
But when individuals are bringing cases

01:06:24.090 --> 01:06:26.580
against refugee receiving states

01:06:26.580 --> 01:06:28.307
for violations of their rights,

01:06:28.307 --> 01:06:30.122
the CEDAW committee is less able

01:06:30.122 --> 01:06:33.410
to recognize that and to
vindicate those rights

01:06:33.410 --> 01:06:35.423
in the migration control context.

01:06:37.690 --> 01:06:41.430
- Yeah, the other thing is that, yeah.

01:06:41.430 --> 01:06:42.363
I guess,

01:06:43.320 --> 01:06:48.320
I mean, when you ask the
question to the people attending

01:06:50.890 --> 01:06:51.753
this seminar,

01:06:54.100 --> 01:06:55.333
probably your,

01:06:56.560 --> 01:06:59.440
I mean, it's not representative of what

01:06:59.440 --> 01:07:03.288
the majority of the US

01:07:03.288 --> 01:07:05.080
people are thinking about

01:07:06.030 --> 01:07:10.223
because yeah as you said, in
other countries, you think,

01:07:11.540 --> 01:07:14.440
I mean, international law sort of

01:07:16.820 --> 01:07:21.820
protecting you or some of the
last resort of some of the

01:07:24.700 --> 01:07:28.710
protection of rights
then you start more like

01:07:28.710 --> 01:07:31.153
an invasion of their own sovereign.

01:07:32.977 --> 01:07:35.170
- And it matches my intuition
as a model that I think

01:07:35.170 --> 01:07:38.100
a lot of people that are sort of

01:07:39.120 --> 01:07:42.750
already have an anti-immigrant
anti-refugee disposition

01:07:42.750 --> 01:07:44.900
also have a deep skepticism of

01:07:44.900 --> 01:07:49.103
international law writ large
and so I think sort of,

01:07:50.230 --> 01:07:52.170
it seems to me that like in at least

01:07:52.170 --> 01:07:54.520
the US political context that

01:07:54.520 --> 01:07:58.150
I don't think international
migration law is like

01:07:58.150 --> 01:08:01.050
seen as a lever likely to
change a lot of things, I guess.

01:08:02.980 --> 01:08:05.570
- Well, if I could follow up
too, on the Libya example,

01:08:05.570 --> 01:08:07.950
I think like from the
north African context,

01:08:07.950 --> 01:08:11.280
the fact that that women's
case was adjudicated against

01:08:11.280 --> 01:08:13.870
Libya and not against,

01:08:13.870 --> 01:08:18.278
the European Union or Italy
for initiating the pushbacks,

01:08:18.278 --> 01:08:20.323
like pushbacks or army,

01:08:21.720 --> 01:08:24.410
Libyan defense or
militias, or all of that,

01:08:24.410 --> 01:08:26.920
like creating those particular contexts,

01:08:26.920 --> 01:08:31.200
I think sort of under,
like underscores the point

01:08:31.200 --> 01:08:33.900
that people feel like a suspicion, right.

01:08:33.900 --> 01:08:37.430
Of international law as still really

01:08:37.430 --> 01:08:40.860
serving European interests
or Euro American interests

01:08:40.860 --> 01:08:45.860
over global interests or being
selective about those maybe-

01:08:47.540 --> 01:08:49.210
- And I think, yeah.

01:08:49.210 --> 01:08:51.630
I would agree with the broad
thrust of that analysis.

01:08:51.630 --> 01:08:54.160
And I think that the case I
talked about towards the end,

01:08:54.160 --> 01:08:56.853
Ms. Abdi Osman's case is
a perfect example of that

01:08:56.853 --> 01:08:58.870
because a European system,

01:08:58.870 --> 01:09:01.250
which was predicated in deterrence,

01:09:01.250 --> 01:09:03.430
which was predicated
in containing refugees

01:09:03.430 --> 01:09:07.830
in particular areas in
Europe, which as I said,

01:09:07.830 --> 01:09:09.630
I kind of went through
the facts of that case

01:09:09.630 --> 01:09:12.590
in a lot of detail, 'cause I
wanted to really make the point

01:09:12.590 --> 01:09:14.270
that on two occasions,

01:09:14.270 --> 01:09:16.770
all of these rights protected
by CEDAW had been violated

01:09:16.770 --> 01:09:19.000
and yet the committee
says we have no evidence

01:09:19.000 --> 01:09:20.080
that if she's going to be returned,

01:09:20.080 --> 01:09:22.110
she would face those violations.

01:09:22.110 --> 01:09:24.610
What do they need then actually
under those circumstances,

01:09:24.610 --> 01:09:26.940
what possibly could she
have presented to them?

01:09:26.940 --> 01:09:29.590
And so we might see that
case as an example of

01:09:29.590 --> 01:09:32.730
migration control imperatives
within the European Union

01:09:32.730 --> 01:09:35.670
and this particular EU model
of returning individuals

01:09:35.670 --> 01:09:37.490
to the first country where
they've claimed asylum

01:09:37.490 --> 01:09:39.930
being vindicated by the
committee in this respect,

01:09:39.930 --> 01:09:42.580
because there's no
challenge available to her.

01:09:42.580 --> 01:09:44.370
She can't, or, it's very difficult

01:09:44.370 --> 01:09:46.575
for her to do so through
European Union mechanisms

01:09:46.575 --> 01:09:49.340
and the one mechanism we might
think should be available

01:09:49.340 --> 01:09:51.600
to her as someone who's
experienced sexual violence,

01:09:51.600 --> 01:09:54.120
torture and gender based violence, CEDAW

01:09:54.120 --> 01:09:56.108
the committee doesn't take the role

01:09:56.108 --> 01:09:59.174
that has been given to
it in international law

01:09:59.174 --> 01:10:01.290
and I think that's really troubling.

01:10:01.290 --> 01:10:02.790
When I discuss this research,

01:10:02.790 --> 01:10:04.910
I'm normally discussing it in countries

01:10:04.910 --> 01:10:06.340
where individuals have the opportunity

01:10:06.340 --> 01:10:08.890
to use the mechanisms
that I'm talking about.

01:10:08.890 --> 01:10:10.530
And so it's very different
when I speak to you

01:10:10.530 --> 01:10:12.570
because it's much more
abstract potentially

01:10:12.570 --> 01:10:14.820
depending on where you might go and work

01:10:14.820 --> 01:10:16.483
after your studies here.

01:10:17.510 --> 01:10:20.060
And so that often kind
of presents a particular

01:10:20.060 --> 01:10:21.200
kind of conflict for me because

01:10:21.200 --> 01:10:22.940
a lot of the time I will point to

01:10:22.940 --> 01:10:25.030
particular things and
states and say, look,

01:10:25.030 --> 01:10:27.590
this, the CEDAW committee has
impugned as discriminatory.

01:10:27.590 --> 01:10:29.270
So when I talk to legal
activists, I'll say,

01:10:29.270 --> 01:10:30.927
why don't you bring a case?

01:10:30.927 --> 01:10:33.650
You use really clear
recommendations on this point

01:10:33.650 --> 01:10:35.500
and you have, for example,

01:10:35.500 --> 01:10:37.750
a group of individuals
who require protection,

01:10:37.750 --> 01:10:40.450
so you should consider
this international remedy.

01:10:40.450 --> 01:10:42.520
So on the one hand, sometimes
when I'm talking to audiences,

01:10:42.520 --> 01:10:45.140
I say, use the remedy,
but it has this problem.

01:10:45.140 --> 01:10:46.070
Whereas when I'm talking to you,

01:10:46.070 --> 01:10:47.520
I can't even say use the remedy,

01:10:47.520 --> 01:10:51.940
I'm painting a picture of an
international regime that has

01:10:51.940 --> 01:10:53.720
potentially only
tangential interest to you

01:10:53.720 --> 01:10:56.470
or tangential impact on the
work that you may be doing.

01:10:58.250 --> 01:11:02.587
- There is a question
on the chat about how,

01:11:07.368 --> 01:11:09.880
if the gender and COVID-19 guidance

01:11:09.880 --> 01:11:11.980
from international bodies has resulted

01:11:11.980 --> 01:11:15.330
in any shift in policies, because

01:11:15.330 --> 01:11:18.903
the problems on the ground
on gender are very real.

01:11:20.530 --> 01:11:23.760
- Ask about how guys
are initiating policies.

01:11:23.760 --> 01:11:25.040
Yes.

01:11:25.040 --> 01:11:25.873
I

01:11:26.860 --> 01:11:31.860
don't know a CEDAW rooted
answer to that question,

01:11:33.030 --> 01:11:34.610
other than that

01:11:34.610 --> 01:11:36.760
the convention protects equal access

01:11:36.760 --> 01:11:39.960
to health care and maternity
services, for example,

01:11:39.960 --> 01:11:41.930
and there has been
successful litigation where

01:11:41.930 --> 01:11:43.873
women's rights, for example,

01:11:45.395 --> 01:11:50.330
to healthcare around having
healthy children was denied,

01:11:51.510 --> 01:11:53.750
particularly on intersexual
grounds, such as

01:11:53.750 --> 01:11:56.230
race and being a member
of an indigenous group.

01:11:56.230 --> 01:11:58.770
There's kind of some really
interesting jurisprudence

01:11:58.770 --> 01:11:59.860
of the CEDAW committee on that

01:11:59.860 --> 01:12:02.230
doesn't relate to COVID-19 though.

01:12:02.230 --> 01:12:05.250
The only specific guidance
I can think of would be from

01:12:05.250 --> 01:12:08.660
UNHCR, which has like a data portal

01:12:08.660 --> 01:12:12.730
where it's produced kind of an analysis of

01:12:12.730 --> 01:12:14.610
either restricted or
non-restricted measures

01:12:14.610 --> 01:12:17.670
adopted by states in relation to COVID-19

01:12:17.670 --> 01:12:20.830
because we're aware that the
pandemic is used by states

01:12:20.830 --> 01:12:22.620
as an opportunity to kind of entrench

01:12:22.620 --> 01:12:25.923
or further entrench
policies of immobility.

01:12:27.260 --> 01:12:30.805
I agree that that presents a
series of issues around gender,

01:12:30.805 --> 01:12:32.570
and particularly in relation
to gender based violence

01:12:32.570 --> 01:12:35.533
but I can't think of a kind
of clear document or source

01:12:35.533 --> 01:12:37.670
that I can point you to.

01:12:37.670 --> 01:12:40.090
I've got Sarah's question now and there

01:12:40.090 --> 01:12:41.490
it's characteristically challenging.

01:12:41.490 --> 01:12:42.863
So thank you for that.

01:12:44.550 --> 01:12:46.890
So have there been any

01:12:47.940 --> 01:12:49.870
interaction between the
committees reasoning,

01:12:49.870 --> 01:12:52.900
the European Court of Human
Rights and other treaty bodies?

01:12:52.900 --> 01:12:54.560
And there has been.

01:12:54.560 --> 01:12:57.130
So the European Court of Human Rights

01:12:57.130 --> 01:12:59.760
regularly refers to the
work of the CEDAW committee

01:12:59.760 --> 01:13:03.290
in its cases on violence and
discrimination against women.

01:13:03.290 --> 01:13:04.950
And there are kind of certain key cases

01:13:04.950 --> 01:13:07.740
such as Opus versus Turkey, for example,

01:13:07.740 --> 01:13:10.420
where the court differs
to the CEDAW committee's

01:13:10.420 --> 01:13:12.950
definition of violence and
discrimination against women

01:13:12.950 --> 01:13:16.450
and says that CEDAW is the
specialist legal regime

01:13:16.450 --> 01:13:18.650
that regional courts should turn to

01:13:18.650 --> 01:13:20.300
when adjudicating on, for example,

01:13:20.300 --> 01:13:22.370
article 14 non-discrimination cases

01:13:22.370 --> 01:13:24.103
involving gender based violence.

01:13:26.380 --> 01:13:30.570
Has there been any interaction
here when we think about

01:13:30.570 --> 01:13:33.860
challenges to Dublin returns
because you're right,

01:13:33.860 --> 01:13:37.490
Abdi Osman is very similar
to a number of cases

01:13:37.490 --> 01:13:38.430
where individuals have gone

01:13:38.430 --> 01:13:40.180
to the European Court of Human Rights

01:13:40.180 --> 01:13:44.330
to challenge their removal
from one EU country to another

01:13:44.330 --> 01:13:46.890
and what they've normally done
is relied on article three,

01:13:46.890 --> 01:13:48.460
which is the prohibition of torture,

01:13:48.460 --> 01:13:51.040
inhuman and degrading treatment.

01:13:51.040 --> 01:13:54.790
And we might want to think
about why Ms. Abdi Osman

01:13:54.790 --> 01:13:56.600
brought a case to the CEDAW committee

01:13:56.600 --> 01:13:58.810
and not to the European
Court of Human Rights

01:13:58.810 --> 01:14:01.410
and I don't know the answer to that.

01:14:01.410 --> 01:14:02.243
I think that it's
interesting to think about

01:14:02.243 --> 01:14:04.270
if you have more than
one international court

01:14:04.270 --> 01:14:07.230
or tribunal to go to why you
would choose a UN treaty body

01:14:07.230 --> 01:14:09.463
and not a regional court.

01:14:10.690 --> 01:14:13.180
It may well be that the court's
reasoning is so restrictive

01:14:13.180 --> 01:14:15.240
when it comes to things
like Dublin returns

01:14:15.240 --> 01:14:17.340
that she thought that the CEDAW committee,

01:14:17.340 --> 01:14:20.300
because her case was so,

01:14:20.300 --> 01:14:22.280
involved gender based
violence in every point of

01:14:22.280 --> 01:14:26.290
her journey and was so kind of,

01:14:26.290 --> 01:14:28.230
it's almost hard to approach it without

01:14:28.230 --> 01:14:30.520
that kind of non-discrimination
gender-based violence lens

01:14:30.520 --> 01:14:32.910
that she thought the CEDAW
committee would vindicate

01:14:32.910 --> 01:14:36.450
her rights more than the
European court would do.

01:14:36.450 --> 01:14:39.040
But you're absolutely right
to point to those cases

01:14:39.040 --> 01:14:40.911
at the European Court of Justice

01:14:40.911 --> 01:14:43.730
and at the European Court of Human Rights.

01:14:43.730 --> 01:14:47.570
And obviously MSS is one
and Terra Cal is another,

01:14:47.570 --> 01:14:49.751
where you see the two courts engaged in a

01:14:49.751 --> 01:14:52.330
kind of transnational conversation about

01:14:52.330 --> 01:14:53.990
the circumstances in which individuals

01:14:53.990 --> 01:14:56.560
can be returned to either Italy or Greece

01:14:56.560 --> 01:14:58.810
and the treatment that
they would face there.

01:14:58.810 --> 01:15:01.420
The committee does not draw
on any of that discussion.

01:15:01.420 --> 01:15:04.840
It does recount kind of
paragraph after paragraph

01:15:04.840 --> 01:15:06.810
of kind of NGO evidence about

01:15:06.810 --> 01:15:09.000
the treatment of asylum seekers in Italy,

01:15:09.000 --> 01:15:10.450
but then it doesn't again,

01:15:10.450 --> 01:15:14.080
link that to its general recommendation

01:15:14.080 --> 01:15:16.380
on the treatment of asylum
seekers and refugees.

01:15:16.380 --> 01:15:18.373
So again, there's this, this mismatch.

01:15:20.540 --> 01:15:23.680
And I would agree entirely
that returns to Italy

01:15:23.680 --> 01:15:27.300
violations are only very rarely found

01:15:27.300 --> 01:15:29.010
in comparison with returns to Greece.

01:15:29.010 --> 01:15:30.320
Although the standard of treatment

01:15:30.320 --> 01:15:32.093
was quite similar in the two cases.

01:15:33.640 --> 01:15:37.890
- Okay, so thank you
very much, Dr. Briddick.

01:15:37.890 --> 01:15:40.290
So if you join me as well, and

01:15:41.590 --> 01:15:44.570
so hopefully at another
time we might be able

01:15:44.570 --> 01:15:48.140
to have you here around and

01:15:48.140 --> 01:15:52.090
this is our last seminar for the fall,

01:15:52.090 --> 01:15:56.470
and we'll see you back
in the spring next year.

01:15:56.470 --> 01:15:57.793
Thank you very much then.

